ed reservoirs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Glutamate-semialdehyde cyclization to pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 6-8; anoxic, locally UV-shielded water; reversible ring-chain equilibrium.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The alpha-amino group condenses with the terminal aldehyde, eliminating water and generating the pyrroline-5-carboxylic-acid ring system.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The spontaneous relationship between glutamate semialdehyde and pyrroline-5-carboxylate is well-established chemistry and does not require an enzyme. The speculative component is upstream abiotic delivery of sufficient semialdehyde and its survival in the proposed setting. Ring-chain equilibration and possible stereochemical erosion remain relevant.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven reduction of the cyclic imine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a proposed hydrogenation surface\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm total, with a locally H2-enriched anoxic headspace\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"exposed metallic sites on awaruite; candidate catalyst\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 5-8; sulfide-poor mineral interface. Free cyanide must be sufficiently depleted or dispersed to avoid severe metal poisoning. Such compatibility is not established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface activation of hydrogen supplies hydrogen equivalents to the ring C=N bond, giving the saturated pyrrolidine nitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Metal-catalyzed imine hydrogenation is established reaction-class chemistry. Its operation on this substrate using natural awaruite at the stated surface conditions is speculative, not experimentally asserted. Nitrile reduction, cyanide poisoning, mineral passivation, and inadequate H2 supply are substantial competing limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Net nitrile hydration in the cyclic-imine pool\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"aqueous acid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic hydration episode, approximately pH 1-3, followed by buffering. Cyclic imines can open during hydrolysis and re-form afterward.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The nitrile is hydrated to a carboxamide. The cyclic structures denote net entry and exit species, not a requirement that the imine ring remain closed throughout.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a speculative net transformation. Acid promotes both nitrile hydration and imine hydrolysis, so preservation of a continuously closed ring cannot be assumed. This formal pathway may experimentally collapse onto the open-chain hydration-and-recyclization branch; it is not evidence for an independently demonstrated mechanism.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven reduction of the cyclic imine amide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a proposed hydrogenation surface\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm total, with a locally H2-enriched anoxic headspace\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"exposed metallic sites on awaruite; candidate catalyst\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Buffered approximately pH 5-8; shaded, sulfide-poor interface; limited free cyanide.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogen addition across the cyclic C=N bond forms prolinamide without requiring reduction of the carboxamide carbonyl.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"An imine is ordinarily more readily hydrogenated than an amide, making the proposed chemoselectivity reasonable. Nevertheless, this specific natural-mineral implementation is speculative, and no yield or rate is established. Upstream configuration is tracked rather than generated selectively by the catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Net amide hydrolysis in the pyrroline pool\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"aqueous acid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 1-3 during hydrolysis; subsequent buffering permits cyclic-imine re-formation. Ammonia is mainly ammonium during the acidic stage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carboxamide hydrolysis generates a carboxylic acid and ammonia, with concurrent reversible opening and closing of the imine ring.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis is chemically supported as a reaction class, but clean conversion of this unstable cyclic-imine pool is speculative. The reaction is explicitly a net edge: the actual microscopic route may pass through open-chain amino aldehydes already represented elsewhere. Hydrolysis versus decomposition is a major kinetic uncertainty.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven pyrroline-5-carboxylic-acid reduction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a proposed hydrogenation surface\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm total, with a locally H2-enriched anoxic headspace\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"exposed metallic sites on awaruite; candidate catalyst\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 5-8; anoxic, sulfide-poor, locally shaded interface; substrate adsorption must not completely inhibit hydrogen activation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogenation of the ring imine produces the saturated secondary-amine ring of proline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reduction of this imine skeleton to proline is chemically well founded. A prebiotic H2/awaruite implementation under these conditions remains speculative. The S-configured precursor can yield L-proline, but no asymmetric induction is supplied, and the upstream racemic population implies corresponding D-proline formation outside the expanded target branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Prolinonitrile hydration to prolinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"aqueous acid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 1-3; anoxic aqueous heating. The saturated ring avoids the imine-opening problem of the unsaturated branches.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-assisted hydration converts the nitrile group into a carboxamide while retaining the pyrrolidine framework.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a conventional aminonitrile-to-amide transformation and is structurally less fragile than hydrolysis of an imine-bearing intermediate. Its useful rate in a natural pool remains unestablished, so the environmental implementation is speculative. Retro-Strecker loss and stereochemical erosion must still be assessed rather than excluded.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Prolinamide hydrolysis to L-proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"aqueous acid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 1-3; prolonged aqueous heating may be necessary. Product protonation changes on subsequent buffering.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the carboxamide releases ammonia and produces the proline carboxylic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis provides a chemically ordinary terminal step without requiring oxygen or enzymes. Its prebiotic throughput at these temperatures is not established and may be rate-limiting. L-proline is obtained from the tracked S component; this step is neither a resolution nor a source of enantiomeric excess.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron alternative for cyclic imine nitrile reduction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"native iron surface, consumed as reductant rather than acting as a catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, mildly acidic microenvironment, approximately pH 3-6; freshly exposed meteoritic or other native-iron grains. Proton coefficients are implicit.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed interfacial electron transfer and protonation reduce the C=N bond while Fe(0) is oxidized to Fe(II); the net reduction requires two proton equivalents.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a speculative alternative reductant, not a demonstrated mineral synthesis of prolinonitrile. Native iron can supply reducing equivalents, but productive imine reduction may lose to hydrogen evolution, passivation, adsorption, or substrate decomposition. Iron is correctly included as a consumed reactant; abundant dissolved Fe(II) alone is not assumed to perform this reduction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron alternative for cyclic imine amide reduction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"native iron surface, consumed as reductant rather than acting as a catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, approximately pH 3-6; exposed native-iron surface; sulfide and oxide passivation must be limited.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed two-electron, proton-coupled reduction of the cyclic imine yields prolinamide and dissolved Fe(II).\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This substrate-specific geochemical reduction is speculative. It is included as a chemically distinct electron-source alternative, not as evidence that corrosion necessarily produces an amine. Amide retention is plausible relative to imine reduction, but interfacial kinetics and useful selectivity are unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron alternative for pyrroline-carboxylic-acid reduction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"native iron surface, consumed as reductant rather than acting as a catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, approximately pH 3-6; freshly exposed iron. Carboxylate binding, iron corrosion, and ring-chain equilibration affect availability.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed reduction of the pyrroline C=N bond by iron-derived electrons and protons gives proline, with Fe(II) as the redox coproduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a speculative nonenzymatic terminal reduction. Electron balance is feasible, but thermodynamic reducing capacity alone does not establish the required reaction rate or selectivity. No claim is made that native iron produces L-proline preferentially over the mirror-image product.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Reduction of the C4 monoimine before cyanide incorporation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a proposed hydrogenation surface\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm total, with a locally H2-enriched anoxic headspace\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"exposed metallic sites on awaruite; candidate catalyst\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 6-8; sulfide-poor interface. Short effective contact and subsequent departure from the reducing surface would be needed to limit overreduction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogenation of the terminal primary imine gives 4-aminobutanal while formally retaining the aldehyde group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reductive amination is established chemistry, but the required selectivity for this bifunctional substrate on natural metal is speculative and particularly challenging. Aldehyde reduction and subsequent overreduction of the cyclic imine to pyrrolidine can eliminate productive flux. No efficient prebiotic mechanism for stopping at the amino aldehyde is established.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"4-Aminobutanal cyclization to 1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 6-8; anoxic, shaded solution away from strongly hydrogenating surfaces; rapid trapping of the cyclic imine is advantageous.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal amino group attacks the aldehyde intramolecularly; dehydration produces the five-membered cyclic imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amino-aldehyde cyclization to a five-membered imine is chemically well grounded. The speculative prebiotic requirements are sufficient amino-aldehyde supply and persistence of 1-pyrroline before polymerization, ring opening, or further reduction. No oxygen-dependent oxidation of pyrrolidine is required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclic Strecker addition to 1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, approximately pH 6-9; cyanide encounters the imine after the mineral-reduction stage to limit catalyst poisoning. Outputs represent a racemic ensemble.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to the cyclic imine or iminium carbon directly produces pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile, introducing the future carboxyl carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyclic-imine Strecker chemistry supplies a chemically coherent alternative ordering: the ring is assembled before the fifth carbon is introduced. Its integrated prebiotic implementation remains speculative because precursor supply, capture efficiency, and environmental compatibility are unvalidated. Attack on either face gives S and R prolinonitrile; no L-selective step is present.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Open-chain Strecker, cyclization, reduction, then hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The S component of the open-chain aminonitrile cyclizes and is reduced before either nitrile-hydrolysis stage. This avoids carrying an imine through the acidic hydrolysis steps, but depends on unvalidated natural-metal hydrogenation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclization, nitrile hydration, reduction, then amide hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The cyclic nitrile pool undergoes net hydration before imine reduction. Ring opening during hydration means this formal sequence may merge experimentally with an open-chain-amide route.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclization and complete nitrile hydrolysis before reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Both hydrolysis stages precede reduction of pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid. The cyclic species are net pool representatives because acidic conditions can open their imine rings.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Open-chain nitrile hydration before cyclization and reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Acidic hydration first generates the amino aldehyde amide. Buffering permits cyclization, followed by imine reduction and final amide hydrolysis. Productive survival during the first hydration is unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Open-chain hydration, cyclization, amide hydrolysis, then reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"The amide forms before ring closure, but its conversion to the carboxylic acid precedes imine reduction. This is a formal reaction-order variant rather than an independently established prebiotic route.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Complete open-chain hydrolysis through glutamate semialdehyde\",\n      \"description\": \"The aminonitrile is hydrolyzed through the amide to glutamate semialdehyde before buffering allows cyclization. Terminal reduction gives the L component of proline. Hydrolysis must outcompete degradation of reactive open-chain intermediates.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron reduction at the cyclic nitrile stage\",\n      \"description\": \"This alternative-reductant variant replaces H2-driven cyclic-imine reduction with a speculative Fe(0)-consuming step before nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron reduction at the cyclic amide stage\",\n      \"description\": \"Open-chain nitrile hydration and cyclization precede proposed iron-driven imine reduction. Final amide hydrolysis furnishes L-proline from the tracked S population. The iron-reduction step is unvalidated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron reduction after glutamate-semialdehyde cyclization\",\n      \"description\": \"Complete open-chain hydrolysis and subsequent cyclization precede a speculative Fe(0)-driven terminal reduction of pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"C4 reductive amination and ring formation before Strecker addition\",\n      \"description\": \"Reduction first generates 4-aminobutanal, which cyclizes to 1-pyrroline. Cyclic Strecker addition then introduces the fifth carbon, and hydrolysis gives proline. Selective monoimine reduction without aldehyde reduction or pyrroline overreduction is a major unresolved bottleneck.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_016\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_020\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Proline", "overall_strategy": "This is a conditional, connected candidate network, not a demonstrated complete prebiotic synthesis. The entry feedstocks are the small C4 dialdehyde succinaldehyde, ammonia, hydrogen cyanide, water, hydrogen, and optionally native iron. Succinaldehyde availability is an unresolved geochemical bottleneck; its inclusion as a starting material does not establish its early-Earth abundance. Strecker chemistry introduces the fifth carbon and an amino group, five-membered-ring condensation establishes the proline skeleton, and reduction plus nitrile hydrolysis furnish proline. Ten formally distinct pathways are enumerated: six reaction-order variants, three alternative-reductant variants, and one reduction-before-Strecker variant. Several variants may merge experimentally because cyclic imines exchange with open-chain amino aldehydes. They are not ten independently validated prebiotic syntheses. Achiral Strecker reactions produce both configurations: S-configured nodes track the L-producing component without assuming separation or asymmetric selection. Corresponding R coproducts are represented at their formation, but their subsequent mirror-image chemistry is not expanded. The target is therefore L-proline within a racemic product pool, not enantiopure L-proline. All modeled chemistry occurs in anoxic surface geothermal settings with distinct acidic, buffered, and reducing microenvironments. Strong incident UV requires local mineral shading for vulnerable intermediates. Neutral molecular formulas are bookkeeping forms; most protonation equilibria, counterions, hydrates, and degradation products are not expanded. No research findings were supplied, and substrate-specific or geochemical uncertainties are stated in the reaction reasoning.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C4H6O2", "iupac_name": "butane-1,4-dial", "common_name": "succinaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "water", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C4H7NO", "iupac_name": "4-iminobutanal", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C5H8N2O", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-amino-5-oxopentanenitrile", "common_name": nul