tion."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Open-chain Strecker addition with racemic stereogenesis", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, approximately pH 7-9, with overlapping imine and HCN/CN- availability; shaded, concentrated microenvironments. Outputs represent the bulk racemic product distribution."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the planar imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, producing the two enantiomers of an amino aldehyde nitrile.", "reasoning": "Strecker addition is chemically well founded, but this substrate-specific prebiotic implementation is speculative. Cyanohydrin formation at the remaining aldehyde, further Strecker chemistry, and decomposition can divert material. No chiral reagent or surface is specified, so formation of the S intermediate necessarily accompanies the R intermediate; the S branch is not an enantioselective yield claim."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cyclization of the S amino aldehyde nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 6-8 after local buffering; concentrated solution or partially drying films; anoxic and shaded."}, "mechanism": "The amino group attacks the terminal aldehyde intramolecularly. Dehydration of the cyclic carbinolamine yields a five-membered imine bearing a nitrile substituent.", "reasoning": "Five-membered amino-aldehyde cyclization is mechanistically favorable. Nevertheless, accumulation and lifetime of this particular nitrile-bearing cyclic imine are unvalidated here, making its prebiotic use speculative. Ring opening and tautomerization are possible; the S label tracks starting configuration and does not establish resistance to racemization."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Hydration of the open-chain amino nitrile to an amide", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["aqueous acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic geothermal water, approximately pH 1-3; prolonged residence may be required. Protonation of the amino group can suppress immediate ring closure."}, "mechanism": "Acid-assisted water addition to the nitrile gives an imidic intermediate that tautomerizes to the carboxamide.", "reasoning": "Nitrile hydration is established chemistry, but the useful rate and survival of this reactive amino aldehyde under the stated conditions are unknown. This is a speculative, potentially rate-limiting prebiotic step. Amide accumulation is a possible kinetic branch point, not an assumption that hydrolysis stops selectively at the amide."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Hydrolysis of the open-chain amide to glutamate semialdehyde", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["aqueous acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 1-3, followed by buffering before productive cyclization. Released ammonia is predominantly ammonium under the reaction conditions."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed amide hydrolysis replaces the carboxamide amino group with hydroxyl, producing the carboxylic acid and ammonia in neutral-form bookkeeping.", "reasoning": "The functional-group conversion is conventional, but production of an appreciable glutamate-semialdehyde pool by this sequence is speculative. Slow amide hydrolysis must compete with aldehyde degradation and other losses. No assumption of quantitative conversion or complete stereochemical retention is made."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Cyclization of the amino aldehyde amide", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Buffered approximately pH 6-8; anoxic, shaded solution or concentrated films after an acidic hydration episode."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular amino-group addition to the aldehyde and dehydration produce a five-membered cyclic imine while retaining the carboxamide.", "reasoning": "This is a chemically reasonable amino-aldehyde condensation, but its substrate-specific prebiotic kinetics are unestablished. It provides a genuine change in functional-group order relative to cyclization before nitrile hydration. Open-chain and cyclic forms are expected to interconvert rather than behave as permanently separated reservoirs."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Glutamate-semialdehyde cyclization to pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 6-8; anoxic, locally UV-shielded water; reversible ring-chain equilibrium."}, "mechanism": "The alpha-amino group condenses with the terminal aldehyde, eliminating water and generating the pyrroline-5-carboxylic-acid ring system.", "reasoning": "The spontaneous relationship between glutamate semialdehyde and pyrroline-5-carboxylate is well-established chemistry and does not require an enzyme. The speculative component is upstream abiotic delivery of sufficient semialdehyde and its survival in the proposed setting. Ring-chain equilibration and possible stereochemical erosion remain relevant."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrogen-driven reduction of the cyclic imine nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a proposed hydrogenation surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K", "pressure": "1 atm total, with a locally H2-enriched anoxic headspace", "catalyst": "exposed metallic sites on awaruite; candidate catalyst", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 5-8; sulfide-poor mineral interface. Free cyanide must be sufficiently depleted or dispersed to avoid severe metal poisoning. Such compatibility is not established."}, "mechanism": "Surface activation of hydrogen supplies hydrogen equivalents to the ring C=N bond, giving the saturated pyrrolidine nitrile.", "reasoning": "Metal-catalyzed imine hydrogenation is established reaction-class chemistry. Its operation on this substrate using natural awaruite at the stated surface conditions is speculative, not experimentally asserted. Nitrile reduction, cyanide poisoning, mineral passivation, and inadequate H2 supply are substantial competing limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Net nitrile hydration in the cyclic-imine pool", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["aqueous acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic hydration episode, approximately pH 1-3, followed by buffering. Cyclic imines can open during hydrolysis and re-form afterward."}, "mechanism": "The nitrile is hydrated to a carboxamide. The cyclic structures denote net entry and exit species, not a requirement that the imine ring remain closed throughout.", "reasoning": "This is a speculative net transformation. Acid promotes both nitrile hydration and imine hydrolysis, so preservation of a continuously closed ring cannot be assumed. This formal pathway may experimentally collapse onto the open-chain hydration-and-recyclization branch; it is not evidence for an independently demonstrated mechanism."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydrogen-driven reduction of the cyclic imine amide", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a proposed hydrogenation surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K", "pressure": "1 atm total, with a locally H2-enriched anoxic headspace", "catalyst": "exposed metallic sites on awaruite; candidate catalyst", "aqueous": true, "other": "Buffered approximately pH 5-8; shaded, sulfide-poor interface; limited free cyanide."}, "mechanism": "Hydrogen addition across the cyclic C=N bond forms prolinamide without requiring reduction of the carboxamide carbonyl.", "reasoning": "An imine is ordinarily more readily hydrogenated than an amide, making the proposed chemoselectivity reasonable. Nevertheless, this specific natural-mineral implementation is speculative, and no yield or rate is established. Upstream configuration is tracked rather than generated selectively by the catalyst."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Net amide hydrolysis in the pyrroline pool", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["aqueous acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 1-3 during hydrolysis; subsequent buffering permits cyclic-imine re-formation. Ammonia is mainly ammonium during the acidic stage."}, "mechanism": "Carboxamide hydrolysis generates a carboxylic acid and ammonia, with concurrent reversible opening and closing of the imine ring.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis is chemically supported as a reaction class, but clean conversion of this unstable cyclic-imine pool is speculative. The reaction is explicitly a net edge: the actual microscopic route may pass through open-chain amino aldehydes already represented elsewhere. Hydrolysis versus decomposition is a major kinetic uncertainty."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Hydrogen-driven pyrroline-5-carboxylic-acid reduction", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a proposed hydrogenation surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K", "pressure": "1 atm total, with a locally H2-enriched anoxic headspace", "catalyst": "exposed metallic sites on awaruite; candidate catalyst", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 5-8; anoxic, sulfide-poor, locally shaded interface; substrate adsorption must not completely inhibit hydrogen activation."}, "mechanism": "Hydrogenation of the ring imine produces the saturated secondary-amine ring of proline.", "reasoning": "Reduction of this imine skeleton to proline is chemically well founded. A prebiotic H2/awaruite implementation under these conditions remains speculative. The S-configured precursor can yield L-proline, but no asymmetric induction is supplied, and the upstream racemic population implies corresponding D-proline formation outside the expanded target branch."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Prolinonitrile hydration to prolinamide", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["aqueous acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 1-3; anoxic aqueous heating. The saturated ring avoids the imine-opening problem of the unsaturated branches."}, "mechanism": "Acid-assisted hydration converts the nitrile group into a carboxamide while retaining the pyrrolidine framework.", "reasoning": "This is a conventional aminonitrile-to-amide transformation and is structurally less fragile than hydrolysis of an imine-bearing intermediate. Its useful rate in a natural pool remains unestablished, so the environmental implementation is speculative. Retro-Strecker loss and stereochemical erosion must still be assessed rather than excluded."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Prolinamide hydrolysis to L-proline", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["aqueous acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 1-3; prolonged aqueous heating may be necessary. Product protonation changes on subsequent buffering."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of the carboxamide releases ammonia and produces the proline carboxylic acid.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis provides a chemically ordinary terminal step without requiring oxygen or enzymes. Its prebiotic throughput at these temperatures is not established and may be rate-limiting. L-proline is obtained from the tracked S component; this step is neither a resolution nor a source of enantiomeric excess."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Native-iron alternative for cyclic imine nitrile reduction", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_020", "mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["native iron surface, consumed as reductant rather than acting as a catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, mildly acidic microenvironment, approximately pH 3-6; freshly exposed meteoritic or other native-iron grains. Proton coefficients are implicit."}, "mechanism": "Proposed interfacial electron transfer and protonation reduce the C=N bond while Fe(0) is oxidized to Fe(II); the net reduction requires two proton equivalents.", "reasoning": "This is a speculative alternative reductant, not a demonstrated mineral synthesis of prolinonitrile. Native iron can supply reducing equivalents, but productive imine reduction may lose to hydrogen evolution, passivation, adsorption, or substrate decomposition. Iron is correctly included as a consumed reactant; abundant dissolved Fe(II) alone is not assumed to perform this reduction."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Native-iron alternative for cyclic imine amide reduction", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_020", "mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["native iron surface, consumed as reductant rather than acting as a catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, approximately pH 3-6; exposed native-iron surface; sulfide and oxide passivation must be limited."}, "mechanism": "Proposed two-electron, proton-coupled reduction of the cyclic imine yields prolinamide and dissolved Fe(II).", "reasoning": "This substrate-specific geochemical reduction is speculative. It is included as a chemically distinct electron-source alternative, not as evidence that corrosion necessarily produces an amine. Amide retention is plausible relative to imine reduction, but interfacial kinetics and useful selectivity are unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Native-iron alternative for pyrroline-carboxylic-acid reduction", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_020", "mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["native iron surface, consumed as reductant rather than acting as a catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, approximately pH 3-6; freshly exposed iron. Carboxylate binding, iron corrosion, and ring-chain equilibration affect availability."}, "mechanism": "Proposed reduction of the pyrroline C=N bond by iron-derived electrons and protons gives proline, with Fe(II) as the redox coproduct.", "reasoning": "This is a speculative nonenzymatic terminal reduction. Electron balance is feasible, but thermodynamic reducing capacity alone does not establish the required reaction rate or selectivity. No claim is made that native iron produces L-proline preferentially over the mirror-image product."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Reduction of the C4 monoimine before cyanide incorporation", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a proposed hydrogenation surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "1 atm total, with a locally H2-enriched anoxic headspace", "catalyst": "exposed metallic sites on awaruite; candidate catalyst", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 6-8; sulfide-poor interface. Short effective contact and subsequent departure from the reducing surface would be needed to limit overreduction."}, "mechanism": "Hydrogenation of the terminal primary imine gives 4-aminobutanal while formally retaining the aldehyde group.", "reasoning": "Reductive amination is established chemistry, but the required selectivity for this bifunctional substrate on natural metal is speculative and particularly challenging. Aldehyde reduction and subsequent overreduction of the cyclic imine to pyrrolidine can eliminate productive flux. No efficient prebiotic mechanism for stopping at the amino aldehyde is established."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "4-Aminobutanal cyclization to 1-pyrroline", "inputs": ["mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 6-8; anoxic, shaded solution away from strongly hydrogenating surfaces; rapid trapping of the cyclic imine is advantageous."}, "mechanism": "The terminal amino group attacks the aldehyde intramolecularly; dehydration produces the five-membered cyclic imine.", "reasoning": "Amino-aldehyde cyclization to a five-membered imine is chemically well grounded. The speculative prebiotic requirements are sufficient amino-aldehyde supply and persistence of 1-pyrroline before polymerization, ring opening, or further reduction. No oxygen-dependent oxidation of pyrrolidine is required."}, {"id": "rxn_020", "name": "Cyclic Strecker addition to 1-pyrroline", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueo