yanide trapping. No catalyst or kinetic staging establishes that selectivity. The final hydrolyses can furnish the stated target without requiring enantioenrichment.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_018\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Measure amino-aldehyde/pyrroline formation against aminoalcohol, pyrrolidine, and pyrrole formation under the proposed reduction conditions.\",\n        \"Identify an environmentally plausible way to terminate hydrogenation or transfer pyrroline to cyanide-trapping conditions before overreduction.\",\n        \"Demonstrate cyclic Strecker capture and subsequent hydrolysis with realistic HCN retention and without assumed purification.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.6,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"This is a connected, sufficiently populated network with consistent pathway connectivity and credible formal routes to proline. The supplied deterministic check establishes no conservation violations: rxn_002 and rxn_020 require the reported racemate coefficients, and rxn_015 through rxn_017 require two protons. These small coefficients are not chemically suspicious. Reconcile the mol_022 formula/structure annotation by consistently representing one Fe atom with charge +2; this flag does not overturn the balance results. L-proline within a racemate satisfies the stated target, and the explicitly unexpanded mirror-image chemistry is not an isolated subgraph. The principal limitations are mechanistic and environmental: potentially competitive Paal-Knorr pyrrole formation during entry chemistry, competing reactions of the remaining aldehyde, unspecified catalytic activation for every H2 reduction, uncertain productive reduction by Fe(0), and uncertain survival of imine/amino-aldehyde pools during hydrolysis. General acidic, buffered, reducing, and shaded surface microenvironments are plausible, but the supplie