{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T07:11:37.563645", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: L-Proline\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.6\n- Feedback: This is a connected, sufficiently populated network with consistent pathway connectivity and credible formal routes to proline. The supplied deterministic check establishes no conservation violations: rxn_002 and rxn_020 require the reported racemate coefficients, and rxn_015 through rxn_017 require two protons. These small coefficients are not chemically suspicious. Reconcile the mol_022 formula/structure annotation by consistently representing one Fe atom with charge +2; this flag does not overturn the balance results. L-proline within a racemate satisfies the stated target, and the explicitly unexpanded mirror-image chemistry is not an isolated subgraph. The principal limitations are mechanistic and environmental: potentially competitive Paal-Knorr pyrrole formation during entry chemistry, competing reactions of the remaining aldehyde, unspecified catalytic activation for every H2 reduction, uncertain productive reduction by Fe(0), and uncertain survival of imine/amino-aldehyde pools during hydrolysis. General acidic, buffered, reducing, and shaded surface microenvironments are plausible, but the supplied entries do not establish operating temperatures, pH windows, H2 availability, active catalyst identities, residence times, or transfer with adequate intermediate retention. Succinaldehyde is accepted as a conditional starting input; its geochemical supply remains unestablished rather than being treated as a conservation defect. No research findings were supplied, so no separate literature-alignment penalty is applied. Route count, hub count, and environment order were not scoring criteria. P1 is the best conditional design, but the network requires substantial kinetic, selectivity, and environmental validation and does not meet the 0.9 network approval threshold.\n- Issues: ['This is the strongest conditional route: reducing the cyclic imine before hydrolysis limits exposure of the reactive imine/amino-aldehyde pool to hydrolytic conditions. Entry chemistry must nevertheless compete with pyrrole-forming Paal-Knorr chemistry, cyanohydrin formation, and other dialdehyde reactions. For rxn_008, H2 is a reductant but does not establish an effective hydrogenation without an active catalyst and suitable conditions. The final hydrolyses are chemically credible and would furnish L-proline within the acknowledged racemate.', 'The route is connected and chemically intelligible. rxn_009 can legitimately represent net hydration through an exchanging cyclic/open-chain pool; an intact cyclic imine need not persist throughout. The uncertainty is whether nitrile hydration preserves enough of that pool for subsequent reduction. rxn_010 also lacks an identified H2 catalyst and operating window. Prolinamide hydrolysis is a credible final transformation, but does not resolve the upstream kinetic and selectivity gaps.', 'Complete hydrolysis before reduction gives a valid formal route through pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid. However, nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis must proceed while the imine/amino-aldehyde pool remains recoverable. Acid-induced ring opening is not itself a failure, but irreversible decomposition and inadequate hydrolysis rates remain unresolved. The final imine reduction is chemically plausible with suitable catalysis; the supplied record does not establish that H2 can accomplish it in the stated setting.', 'Hydration followed by five-membered-ring condensation is structurally coherent. The principal additional uncertainty is selective nitrile hydration while retaining the reactive amino-aldehyde skeleton. Ring-chain equilibration is allowed and is not itself a defect in this pathway. Once the cyclic amide is available, reduction and prolinamide hydrolysis provide a sensible route to the target, but the H2 reduction remains insufficiently specified.', 'All intermediates connect correctly to proline, but the route depends on successful nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis before the reactive imine is reduced. The network does not establish a hydrolysis window that combines useful rates with preservation of the amino-aldehyde/cyclic-imine pool. Subsequent H2 reduction requires catalytic activation that is not identified. These are kinetic and environmental uncertainties, not atom-balance violations.', 'Glutamate-semialdehyde cyclization to pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid is a particularly well-founded transformation. The difficult part is delivering intact semialdehyde through nitrile and amide hydrolysis rather than assuming that conditions adequate for hydrolysis preserve the reactive carbonyl-containing substrate. Allowing cyclic/open-chain exchange removes any requirement to isolate each open-chain intermediate, but does not establish survival or productive rates. Final H2 reduction remains catalyst-dependent.', 'The iron reduction is atom- and charge-balanced with two protons, as reported by the deterministic check. That establishes electron bookkeeping, not productive imine reduction. Native iron is a stated input, but its surface activity, corrosion rate, passivation, and selectivity toward the imine rather than competing processes are unspecified. If prolinonitrile is obtained, the remaining hydrolyses provide a credible route to the target.', 'This evaluation reaches the cyclic amide through cyclization followed by rxn_009. Hydration through an exchanging ring-chain pool is plausible, but substrate recovery is unestablished. The balanced Fe(0) reduction then requires evidence that useful reduction competes successfully with corrosion and surface deactivation. Prolinamide hydrolysis is chemically sound, but productive delivery of prolinamide under the proposed conditions remains uncertain.', 'Consistent with the pathway description, this evaluation follows hydrolysis through glutamate semialdehyde and then rxn_007. The ring closure is strong chemistry, but recovery of its precursor through both hydrolysis stages is uncertain. The final iron-mediated reduction is correctly balanced yet lacks a demonstrated productive mechanism or operating window. Acidic conditions that expose fresh iron can also promote corrosion, so substrate reduction cannot be inferred from iron dissolution alone.', 'Cyclization of gamma-aminobutyraldehyde and cyclic Strecker addition provide a credible route to racemic prolinonitrile. The bottleneck is rxn_018: reduction must favor the monoimine over the remaining aldehyde, and the resulting pyrroline must escape further reduction to pyrrolidine before cyanide trapping. No catalyst or kinetic staging establishes that selectivity. The final hydrolyses can furnish the stated target without requiring enantioenrichment.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-Proline\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Ten graph-distinct, conditional route variants converge on racemic prolinonitrile and then prolinamide. Hydrolysis produces L-proline together with D-proline; no enantioenrichment or resolution is claimed. P1-P4 use cyclization of 4-aminobutanal followed by cyclic-imine Strecker addition, avoiding dialdehyde selectivity and catalytic hydrogenation problems. P5-P10 are less secure alternatives using succinaldehyde, Strecker addition, cyclization, and explicitly catalyst-dependent hydrogenation. In every productive route, the ring is saturated before nitrile or amide hydrolysis: no route assumes that an unreduced amino-aldehyde/imine pool survives prolonged hydrolysis. Acid-catalyzed and hydroxide-promoted hydrolysis provide alternative mechanisms; their combinations constitute route variants, not ten independent, experimentally validated prebiotic syntheses. Both C4 feedstocks are conditional inputs whose sustained geochemical supply remains unresolved. Accordingly, this is a complete graph for the declared conditional feeds, not a demonstrated closed synthesis from universally available inorganic feedstocks. Proposed conditions and residence times are screening windows, not measured optima or promised yields. A competing Paal-Knorr sink is included explicitly. Neutral-parent formulas represent compounds across protonation states; pH reservoirs and counterions are not expanded into separate speciation reactions. environment_formed records a representative formation environment when alternatives exist. D-proline and pyrrole are terminal coproduct/sink species assigned the schema's intermediate role because no coproduct role is available.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"butane-1,4-dial\",\n      \"common_name\": \"succinaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H6O2/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h3-4H,1-2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-oxopentanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3,4-dihydro-2H-pyrrole-2-carbonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"racemic prolinonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"racemic prolinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-proline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2R)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D-proline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m1/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"gamma-aminobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3,4-dihydro-2H-pyrrole\",\n      \"common_name\": \"1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H5N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1H-pyrrole\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyrrole\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H5N/c1-2-4-5-3-1/h1-5H\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional mono-Strecker capture of succinaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_002\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, UV-shielded geothermal seep pool; proposed pH 7.5-8.5. Screen dilute, millimolar dialdehyde with limiting ammonia/cyanide delivery and immediate coupling to rxn_002. Proposed observation window: minutes to several hours, rather than prolonged storage. Concentrations and dosing are experimental variables, not an established selective natural regime.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine/iminium species at one aldehyde terminus. Cyanide addition gives an alpha-aminonitrile while retaining the second aldehyde; water is eliminated in the net Strecker transformation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The carbon and nitrogen connectivity is appropriate for proline, but the substrate-specific prebiotic implementation is speculative. Mono-functionalization must compete with cyanohydrin formation, reactions at the second aldehyde, oligomerization, and the explicit Paal-Knorr sink rxn_011. Limiting reagent delivery is a selectivity hypothesis, not proof that these losses are suppressed. This branch is admissible only if experiments demonstrate recoverable mol_006/mol_007 and a usable residual-cyanide level. Succinaldehyde supply is also conditional.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Five-membered imine formation from the amino-aldehyde nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same shaded, anoxic reaction volume as rxn_001; proposed pH 7.5-8.5. Treat open-chain and cyclic species as an exchanging pool, not separately stored reagents. Couple directly to an adjacent hydrogenation surface. Any transfer should avoid acidification and be tested on a sub-minute to minute timescale.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The amino group attacks the remaining aldehyde intramolecularly. Carbinolamine dehydration forms a five-membered cyclic imine retaining the nitrile substituent.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Five-membered intramolecular imine formation is chemically well founded. Nevertheless, its equilibrium population and lifetime for this nitrile-bearing substrate under the proposed conditions are unestablished. No claim is made that the cyclic imine can be isolated, stored, or transported between distant environments. Productive hydrogenation must outrun hydrolysis and irreversible side reactions within the same local flow system.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional Ni-catalyzed hydrogenation of the cyclic imine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Accessible metallic Ni(0) domains on freshly exposed nickel-iron alloy surfaces\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm total; proposed H2 partial pressure 0.2-0.8 bar in a local seep-gas pocket\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Exposed Ni(0) sites on a nickel-iron alloy; bulk alloy presence alone is insufficient\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, sulfide-poor, serpentinization-fed geothermal seep; proposed pH 7-8.5. Local H2 enrichment is required and is not attributed to the bulk atmosphere. Proposed initial residence-time screen: 5-60 minutes. Free cyanide must be below an experimentally determined poisoning threshold, and the catalyst must retain exposed metallic sites. Neither cyanide removal nor adequate natural surface area is assumed. Remove the saturated product from the metal surface before hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metallic nickel dissociates H2. Adsorbed hydrogen reduces the ring C=N bond to C-N, producing the saturated pyrrolidine nitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ni(0), rather than H2 alone, supplies a chemically identified hydrogen-activation mechanism. The natural-alloy implementation remains speculative: low-pressure activity, available surface area, cyanide/sulfide poisoning, and preferential C=N reduction over nitrile reduction require measurement. Short exposure and mild temperature are proposed selectivity controls, not established solutions. If the necessary free-cyanide level or reduction rate cannot be attained without destroying the imine pool, this route is closed. No Fe(0) corrosion is treated as evidence of productive reduction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional Pt-catalyzed hydrogenation of the cyclic imine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Exposed Pt(0) surfaces on native platinum grains\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm total; proposed H2 partial pressure 0.1-0.8 bar in a local seep-gas pocket\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Accessible native Pt(0), not dissolved platinum salts or an assumed laboratory-supported catalyst\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded, sulfide-poor seep interface; proposed pH 6.5-8.5. Screen minutes to several hours while monitoring nitrile retention. This route requires an unusual local concentration of exposed native platinum with adequate active surface area. Residual free cyanide and sulfur poisoning must be measured; their spontaneous removal is not assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pt(0) activates H2 and transfers surface hydrogen to the cyclic imine bond, leaving the nitrile intact if the kinetic selectivity window is favorable.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Platinum provides a credible low-temperature H2-activation mechanism and is therefore a chemically distinct catalyst alternative to nickel. However, substrate-specific selectivity and a productive native-mineral surface area are not established here. Platinum rarity, poisoning, and possible nitrile hydrogenation make this a geographically restrictive, speculative prebiotic implementation, not a generally available solution. It is retained only as an explicitly conditional alternative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Ring-chain equilibration of 4-aminobutanal to 1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, UV-shielded pool or pore; proposed pH 7.5-8.5. Introduce the amino-aldehyde directly into a reaction volume coupled to rxn_006. Follow equilibration and trapping over minutes to hours. Do not assume isolated amino-aldehyde or pyrroline remains stable during storage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular attack of the terminal amine on the aldehyde forms a five-membered carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to 1-pyrroline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a chemically natural five-membered ring closure and avoids selective reduction of a dialdehyde monoimine. Ring opening is compatible with the route because rxn_006 can trap the cyclic-imine population in the same solution. The principal upstream gap is geochemical production and delivery of 4-aminobutanal; that supply is not established by declaring it a feedstock. Oligomerization and the actual ring-chain distribution remain experimental constraints.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclic-imine Strecker addition to form prolinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same shaded, anoxic pool as rxn_005; proposed pH 7-8.5 and millimolar total cyanide as an initial screening range. No added ammonia is required. Follow product formation over minutes to approximately 12 hours while measuring cyanohydrin and oligomer formation. Cyanide speciation and volatilization must be included in the material budget.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the cyclic imine/iminium carbon, and proton transfer gives pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile. Attack on either face produces the two enantiomers.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyclic-imine Strecker chemistry gives the required proline skeleton directly without H2, a hydrogenation catalyst, or selective monoimine reduction. This is the preferred conditional entry on mechanistic grounds, not a claim of demonstrated geochemical availability. Competing capture of open-chain aldehyde and limited imine abundance can lower yield. No hydrolysis of an unreduced imine is required: the C=N bond is consumed during cyanide addition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed hydration of saturated prolinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions supplied by HCl-bearing volcanic condensate\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"345-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Shaded, anoxic acidic geothermal pool; proposed pH 0-1.5. Initial kinetic screen: 12 hours to 7 days, without assuming completion. Adequate titratable acidity is required because amine protonation consumes acid capacity. Prolinamide may be transient rather than isolable. For a switch to alkaline hydrolysis, transfer only the saturated nitrile/amide pool, not upstream imines.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation promotes water addition. Proton transfers and imidic-acid/amide tautomerization produce prolinamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile-to-amide hydration is an established reaction class, and applying it after ring saturation removes the earlier design's requirement to preserve a reactive amino-aldehyde/imine pool during hydrolysis. The exact rate and product recovery under naturally attainable acidity remain unestablished. Retro-Strecker loss and other degradation must be measured rather than excluded by assumption. Strongly acidic condensate is a conditional local resource, not a universal surface environment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydroxide-promoted hydration of saturated prolinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydroxide ions in alkaline rock-water fluids\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-420 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 100-300 bar; liquid water maintained\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline, anoxic subseafloor or confined geothermal fluid; proposed in-situ pH 10.5-11.5. Initial residence-time screen: 6-72 hours, subject to measured decomposition. Separate the fluid from Ni/Pt hydrogenation surfaces. Entry from a surface pool requires transport of saturated prolinonitrile, not a labile imine. The amide need not accumulate if rxn_010 is faster.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide addition to the nitrile carbon forms an imidate-type intermediate; proton transfer and tautomerization yield the amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a mechanistically distinct hydrolysis alternative to acid activation. Elevated temperature addresses the otherwise slow hydration of an unactivated nitrile, but useful rates and survival of this specific aminonitrile must be demonstrated. The stated pH and temperature are plausible only for a suitable alkaline circulation cell, not for every vent. No mineral is assigned a catalytic role merely because it is present.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid hydrolysis of racemic prolinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions supplied by HCl-bearing volcanic condensate\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, UV-shielded acidic pool; proposed pH 0-1. Initial screen: 1-14 days, with direct measurement of amide cleavage and proline recovery. Acidity must be maintained against amine and ammonia protonation. Actual dissolved products include protonated species; molecule nodes use neutral-parent formulas. A preceding alkaline step requires an explicit fluid transfer and sufficient acid capacity.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbonyl protonation and water addition form a tetrahedral intermediate. C-N cleavage releases the amide nitrogen as ammonia/ammonium and leaves the proline carboxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis is chemically well founded, although its rate in the proposed natural pool is not established. The saturated pyrrolidine ring avoids the imine-survival bottleneck. In an achiral system, the racemic amide gives both L- and D-proline rather than enantiopure L-proline. Listing both enantiomers implies the balanced aggregate stoichiometry of two amide molecules and two water molecules producing one of each proline enantiomer and two ammonia molecules; IDs are intentionally not repeated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline hydrolysis of racemic prolinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydroxide ions in alkaline rock-water fluids\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400-430 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 100-300 bar; liquid water maintained\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide-promoted hydrolysis; sustained fluid alkalinity is required\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic alkaline circulation cell; proposed in-situ pH 10.5-11.5. Initial residence-time screen: 6-72 hours, followed by cooling if conversion is useful. Monitor decarboxylation and other thermal losses. Product carboxylate formation consumes alkalinity in the actual ionic system; the fluid's capacity cannot be treated as unlimited. An acid-to-base sequence requires transport and neutralization of a saturated amide-containing solution.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide attacks the amide carbonyl. Tetrahedral-intermediate collapse and proton transfer yield prolinate and ammonia; the graph records proline in its neutral-parent representation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Base-promoted amide cleavage is a credible alternative to acid hydrolysis. Higher temperature can accelerate cleavage but introduces a competing thermal-degradation budget that requires measurement. Unlike the previous hydrolysis-first designs, no amino-aldehyde or cyclic imine needs to survive this treatment. Both proline enantiomers are produced from the racemic precursor; this reaction does not establish or amplify L-selectivity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing Paal-Knorr diversion of succinaldehyde to pyrrole\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Competes in the same dialdehyde/ammonia pool as rxn_001. Examine pH 6-8.5 and the full productive-route residence time. Acidification, concentration, and prolonged residence may favor diversion; no numerical branching ratio is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia condensation with the 1,4-dialdehyde, ring closure, and net loss of two water molecules produce aromatic pyrrole without molecular oxygen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Paal-Knorr pyrrole formation is a chemically justified competing sink, not an omitted detail or a useful route to proline in this graph. Its explicit inclusion prevents mono-Strecker selectivity from being assumed on structural grounds alone. If this sink dominates, P5-P10 fail regardless of whether their downstream transformations are balanced. P1-P4 avoid this specific dialdehyde/ammonia competition.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclic Strecker entry followed by acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The preferred conditional backbone: 4-aminobutanal equilibrates with 1-pyrroline, cyanide traps the cyclic imine, and acid-mediated nitrile hydration and amide cleavage give L-proline within a racemate. No hydrogenation or prolonged unreduced-imine hydrolysis is required. Feedstock supply and hydrolysis recovery remain unvalidated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclic Strecker entry followed by alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The same direct skeleton-forming entry as P1, followed by transport of saturated prolinonitrile into an alkaline hydrothermal circulation cell. Both hydrolysis stages are hydroxide-promoted. This substitutes a thermal-survival and alkalinity requirement for the strong-acid requirement.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclic Strecker entry with acid hydration and alkaline amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Acid hydration supplies a saturated prolinamide-containing pool, which subsequently enters an alkaline circulation cell for amide cleavage. This is a distinct reaction-node sequence, not a separate carbon-skeleton synthesis. It requires actual fluid transfer, an alkalinity budget, and retention of the saturated intermediate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclic Strecker entry with alkaline hydration and acid amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydroxide-promoted nitrile hydration is followed by discharge into an acidic geothermal pool for amide cleavage. This variant is available only if prolinamide survives transport and the receiving pool has adequate acidity; no spontaneous pH switching at an unchanged site is assumed.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Dialdehyde Strecker entry with nickel reduction and acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional mono-Strecker capture and immediate ring closure precede Ni/H2 reduction. Only the saturated nitrile then encounters prolonged acidic hydrolysis. The pathway fails if Paal-Knorr diversion, cyanide poisoning, inadequate Ni activity, or nitrile over-reduction prevents useful prolinonitrile production.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Dialdehyde Strecker entry with nickel reduction and alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"After the same conditional nickel-mediated reduction as P5, saturated prolinonitrile enters an alkaline hydrothermal compartment separated from the hydrogenation surface. This retains the entry and catalyst uncertainties while replacing acidic hydrolysis with the alkaline mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-reduced entry with acid hydration and alkaline amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Nickel reduction first removes the ring imine. Acid hydration then supplies the saturated amide for later alkaline cleavage. Transfer is confined to the saturated intermediate stage; the unreduced imine is neither stored nor transported between distant environments.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Dialdehyde Strecker entry with platinum reduction and acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Native Pt/H2 replaces nickel at the cyclic-imine reduction step, followed by acidic hydrolysis of the saturated nitrile and amide. This variant requires a rare, sufficiently exposed platinum surface and demonstrated resistance to poisoning under the actual feed composition.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Dialdehyde Strecker entry with platinum reduction and alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional Pt-mediated ring-imine reduction is followed by both alkaline hydrolysis stages. The saturated product is separated from platinum before heating, so the design does not intentionally expose the nitrile to simultaneous hydrogenation and hot hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Platinum-reduced entry with alkaline hydration and acid amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"The Pt-reduced nitrile undergoes alkaline hydration, followed by transfer of a saturated prolinamide-containing fluid into an acidic pool. This tenth graph path is a mixed-hydrolysis variant, not evidence for a tenth independently demonstrated prebiotic synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Proline", "overall_strategy": "Ten graph-distinct, conditional route variants converge on racemic prolinonitrile and then prolinamide. Hydrolysis produces L-proline together with D-proline; no enantioenrichment or resolution is claimed. P1-P4 use cyclization of 4-aminobutanal followed by cyclic-imine Strecker addition, avoiding dialdehyde selectivity and catalytic hydrogenation problems. P5-P10 are less secure alternatives using succinaldehyde, Strecker addition, cyclization, and explicitly catalyst-dependent hydrogenation. In every productive route, the ring is saturated before nitrile or amide hydrolysis: no route assumes that an unreduced amino-aldehyde/imine pool survives prolonged hydrolysis. Acid-catalyzed and hydroxide-promoted hydrolysis provide alternative mechanisms; their combinations constitute route variants, not ten independent, experimentally validated prebiotic syntheses. Both C4 feedstocks are conditional inputs whose sustained geochemical supply remains unresolved. Accordingly, this is a complete graph for the declared conditional feeds, not a demonstrated closed synthesis from universally available inorganic feedstocks. Proposed conditions and residence times are screening windows, not measured optima or promised yields. A competing Paal-Knorr sink is included explicitly. Neutral-parent formulas represent compounds across protonation states; pH reservoirs and counterions are not expanded into separate speciation reactions. environment_formed records a representative formation environment when alternatives exist. D-proline and pyrrole are terminal coproduct/sink species assigned the schema's intermediate role because no coproduct role is available.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C4H6O2", "iupac_name": "butane-1,4-dial", "common_name": "succinaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H6O2/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h3-4H,1-2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C5H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-5-oxopentanenitrile", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C5H6N2", "iupac_name": "3,4-dihydro-2H-pyrrole-2-carbonitrile", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H8N2", "iupac_name": "pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile", "common_name": "racemic prolinonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C5H10N2O", "iupac_name": "pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide", "common_name": "racemic prolinamide", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "L-proline", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "D-proline", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m1/s1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C4H9NO", "iupac_name": "4-aminobutanal", "common_name": "gamma-aminobutyraldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C4H7N", "iupac_name": "3,4-dihydro-2H-pyrrole", "common_name": "1-pyrroline", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C4H5N", "iupac_name": "1H-pyrrole", "common_name": "pyrrole", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H5N/c1-2-4-5-3-1/h1-5H", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Conditional mono-Strecker capture of succinaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, UV-shielded geothermal seep pool; proposed pH 7.5-8.5. Screen dilute, millimolar dialdehyde with limiting ammonia/cyanide delivery and immediate coupling to rxn_002. Proposed observation window: minutes to several hours, rather than prolonged storage. Concentrations and dosing are experimental variables, not an established selective natural regime."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine/iminium species at one aldehyde terminus. Cyanide addition gives an alpha-aminonitrile while retaining the second aldehyde; water is eliminated in the net Strecker transformation.", "reasoning": "The carbon and nitrogen connectivity is appropriate for proline, but the substrate-specific prebiotic implementation is speculative. Mono-functionalization must compete with cyanohydrin formation, reactions at the second aldehyde, oligomerization, and the explicit Paal-Knorr sink rxn_011. Limiting reagent delivery is a selectivity hypothesis, not proof that these losses are suppressed. This branch is admissible only if experiments demonstrate recoverable mol_006/mol_007 and a usable residual-cyanide level. Succinaldehyde supply is also conditional."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Five-membered imine formation from the amino-aldehyde nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Same shaded, anoxic reaction volume as rxn_001; proposed pH 7.5-8.5. Treat open-chain and cyclic species as an exchanging pool, not separately stored reagents. Couple directly to an adjacent hydrogenation surface. Any transfer should avoid acidification and be tested on a sub-minute to minute timescale."}, "mechanism": "The amino group attacks the remaining aldehyde intramolecularly. Carbinolamine dehydration forms a five-membered cyclic imine retaining the nitrile substituent.", "reasoning": "Five-membered intramolecular imine formation is chemically well founded. Nevertheless, its equilibrium population and lifetime for this nitrile-bearing substrate under the proposed conditions are unestablished. No claim is made that the cyclic imine can be isolated, stored, or transported between distant environments. Productive hydrogenation must outrun hydrolysis and irreversible side reactions within the same local flow system."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Conditional Ni-catalyzed hydrogenation of the cyclic imine nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Accessible metallic Ni(0) domains on freshly exposed nickel-iron alloy surfaces"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm total; proposed H2 partial pressure 0.2-0.8 bar in a local seep-gas pocket", "catalyst": "Exposed Ni(0) sites on a nickel-iron alloy; bulk alloy presence alone is insufficient", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, sulfide-poor, serpentinization-fed geothermal seep; proposed pH 7-8.5. Local H2 enrichment is required and is not attributed to the bulk atmosphere. Proposed initial residence-time screen: 5-60 minutes. Free cyanide must be below an experimentally determined poisoning threshold, and the catalyst must retain exposed metallic sites. Neither cyanide removal nor adequate natural surface area is assumed. Remove the saturated product from the metal surface before hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Metallic nickel dissociates H2. Adsorbed hydrogen reduces the ring C=N bond to C-N, producing the saturated pyrrolidine nitrile.", "reasoning": "Ni(0), rather than H2 alone, supplies a chemically identified hydrogen-activation mechanism. The natural-alloy implementation remains speculative: low-pressure activity, available surface area, cyanide/sulfide poisoning, and preferential C=N reduction over nitrile reduction require measurement. Short exposure and mild temperature are proposed selectivity controls, not established solutions. If the necessary free-cyanide level or reduction rate cannot be attained without destroying the imine pool, this route is closed. No Fe(0) corrosion is treated as evidence of productive reduction."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Conditional Pt-catalyzed hydrogenation of the cyclic imine nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Exposed Pt(0) surfaces on native platinum grains"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm total; proposed H2 partial pressure 0.1-0.8 bar in a local seep-gas pocket", "catalyst": "Accessible native Pt(0), not dissolved platinum salts or an assumed laboratory-supported catalyst", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded, sulfide-poor seep interface; proposed pH 6.5-8.5. Screen minutes to several hours while monitoring nitrile retention. This route requires an unusual local concentration of exposed native platinum with adequate active surface area. Residual free cyanide and sulfur poisoning must be measured; their spontaneous removal is not assumed."}, "mechanism": "Pt(0) activates H2 and transfers surface hydrogen to the cyclic imine bond, leaving the nitrile intact if the kinetic selectivity window is favorable.", "reasoning": "Platinum provides a credible low-temperature H2-activation mechanism and is therefore a chemically distinct catalyst alternative to nickel. However, substrate-specific selectivity and a productive native-mineral surface area are not established here. Platinum rarity, poisoning, and possible nitrile hydrogenation make this a geographically restrictive, speculative prebiotic implementation, not a generally available solution. It is retained only as an explicitly conditional alternative."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Ring-chain equilibration of 4-aminobutanal to 1-pyrroline", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, UV-shielded pool or pore; proposed pH 7.5-8.5. Introduce the amino-aldehyde directly into a reaction volume coupled to rxn_006. Follow equilibration and trapping over minutes to hours. Do not assume isolated amino-aldehyde or pyrroline remains stable during storage."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular attack of the terminal amine on the aldehyde forms a five-membered carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to 1-pyrroline.", "reasoning": "This is a chemically natural five-membered ring closure and avoids selective reduction of a dialdehyde monoimine. Ring opening is compatible with the route because rxn_006 can trap the cyclic-imine population in the same solution. The principal upstream gap is geochemical production and delivery of 4-aminobutanal; that supply is not established by declaring it a feedstock. Oligomerization and the actual ring-chain distribution remain experimental constraints."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Cyclic-imine Strecker addition to form prolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Same shaded, anoxic pool as rxn_005; proposed pH 7-8.5 and millimolar total cyanide as an initial screening range. No added ammonia is required. Follow product formation over minutes to approximately 12 hours while measuring cyanohydrin and oligomer formation. Cyanide speciation and volatilization must be included in the material budget."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the cyclic imine/iminium carbon, and proton transfer gives pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile. Attack on either face produces the two enantiomers.", "reasoning": "Cyclic-imine Strecker chemistry gives the required proline skeleton directly without H2, a hydrogenation catalyst, or selective monoimine reduction. This is the preferred conditional entry on mechanistic grounds, not a claim of demonstrated geochemical availability. Competing capture of open-chain aldehyde and limited imine abundance can lower yield. No hydrolysis of an unreduced imine is required: the C=N bond is consumed during cyanide addition."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Acid-catalyzed hydration of saturated prolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions supplied by HCl-bearing volcanic condensate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "345-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Shaded, anoxic acidic geothermal pool; proposed pH 0-1.5. Initial kinetic screen: 12 hours to 7 days, without assuming completion. Adequate titratable acidity is required because amine protonation consumes acid capacity. Prolinamide may be transient rather than isolable. For a switch to alkaline hydrolysis, transfer only the saturated nitrile/amide pool, not upstream imines."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation promotes water addition. Proton transfers and imidic-acid/amide tautomerization produce prolinamide.", "reasoning": "Nitrile-to-amide hydration is an established reaction class, and applying it after ring saturation removes the earlier design's requirement to preserve a reactive amino-aldehyde/imine pool during hydrolysis. The exact rate and product recovery under naturally attainable acidity remain unestablished. Retro-Strecker loss and other degradation must be measured rather than excluded by assumption. Strongly acidic condensate is a conditional local resource, not a universal surface environment."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydroxide-promoted hydration of saturated prolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide ions in alkaline rock-water fluids"], "conditions": {"temperature": "390-420 K", "pressure": "Approximately 100-300 bar; liquid water maintained", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Alkaline, anoxic subseafloor or confined geothermal fluid; proposed in-situ pH 10.5-11.5. Initial residence-time screen: 6-72 hours, subject to measured decomposition. Separate the fluid from Ni/Pt hydrogenation surfaces. Entry from a surface pool requires transport of saturated prolinonitrile, not a labile imine. The amide need not accumulate if rxn_010 is faster."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide addition to the nitrile carbon forms an imidate-type intermediate; proton transfer and tautomerization yield the amide.", "reasoning": "This is a mechanistically distinct hydrolysis alternative to acid activation. Elevated temperature addresses the otherwise slow hydration of an unactivated nitrile, but useful rates and survival of this specific aminonitrile must be demonstrated. The stated pH and temperature are plausible only for a suitable alkaline circulation cell, not for every vent. No mineral is assigned a catalytic role merely because it is present."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Acid hydrolysis of racemic prolinamide", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions supplied by HCl-bearing volcanic condensate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, UV-shielded acidic pool; proposed pH 0-1. Initial screen: 1-14 days, with direct measurement of amide cleavage and proline recovery. Acidity must be maintained against amine and ammonia protonation. Actual dissolved products include protonated species; molecule nodes use neutral-parent formulas. A preceding alkaline step requires an explicit fluid transfer and sufficient acid capacity."}, "mechanism": "Carbonyl protonation and water addition form a tetrahedral intermediate. C-N cleavage releases the amide nitrogen as ammonia/ammonium and leaves the proline carboxyl group.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis is chemically well founded, although its rate in the proposed natural pool is not established. The saturated pyrrolidine ring avoids the imine-survival bottleneck. In an achiral system, the racemic amide gives both L- and D-proline rather than enantiopure L-proline. Listing both enantiomers implies the balanced aggregate stoichiometry of two amide molecules and two water molecules producing one of each proline enantiomer and two ammonia molecules; IDs are intentionally not repeated."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Alkaline hydrolysis of racemic prolinamide", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide ions in alkaline rock-water fluids"], "conditions": {"temperature": "400-430 K", "pressure": "Approximately 100-300 bar; liquid water maintained", "catalyst": "Hydroxide-promoted hydrolysis; sustained fluid alkalinity is required", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic alkaline circulation cell; proposed in-situ pH 10.5-11.5. Initial residence-time screen: 6-72 hours, followed by cooling if conversion is useful. Monitor decarboxylation and other thermal losses. Product carboxylate formation consumes alkalinity in the actual ionic system; the fluid's capacity cannot be treated as unlimited. An acid-to-base sequence requires transport and neutralization of a saturated amide-containing solution."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide attacks the amide carbonyl. Tetrahedral-intermediate collapse and proton transfer yield prolinate and ammonia; the graph records proline in its neutral-parent representation.", "reasoning": "Base-promoted amide cleavage is a credible alternative to acid hydrolysis. Higher temperature can accelerate cleavage but introduces a competing thermal-degradation budget that requires measurement. Unlike the previous hydrolysis-first designs, no amino-aldehyde or cyclic imine needs to survive this treatment. Both proline enantiomers are produced from the racemic precursor; this reaction does not establish or amplify L-selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Competing Paal-Knorr diversion of succinaldehyde to pyrrole", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Competes in the same dialdehyde/ammonia pool as rxn_001. Examine pH 6-8.5 and the full productive-route residence time. Acidification, concentration, and prolonged residence may favor diversion; no numerical branching ratio is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia condensation with the 1,4-dialdehyde, ring closure, and net loss of two water molecules produce aromatic pyrrole without molecular oxygen.", "reasoning": "Paal-Knorr pyrrole formation is a chemically justified competing sink, not an omitted detail or a useful route to proline in this graph. Its explicit inclusion prevents mono-Strecker selectivity from being assumed on structural grounds alone. If this sink dominates, P5-P10 fail regardless of whether their downstream transformations are balanced. P1-P4 avoid this specific dialdehyde/ammonia competition."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Cyclic Strecker entry followed by acidic hydrolysis", "description": "The preferred conditional backbone: 4-aminobutanal equilibrates with 1-pyrroline, cyanide traps the cyclic imine, and acid-mediated nitrile hydration and amide cleavage give L-proline within a racemate. No hydrogenation or prolonged unreduced-imine hydrolysis is required. Feedstock supply and hydrolysis recovery remain unvalidated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Cyclic Strecker entry followed by alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "The same direct skeleton-forming entry as P1, followed by transport of saturated prolinonitrile into an alkaline hydrothermal circulation cell. Both hydrolysis stages are hydroxide-promoted. This substitutes a thermal-survival and alkalinity requirement for the strong-acid requirement.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Cyclic Strecker entry with acid hydration and alkaline amide cleavage", "description": "Acid hydration supplies a saturated prolinamide-containing pool, which subsequently enters an alkaline circulation cell for amide cleavage. This is a distinct reaction-node sequence, not a separate carbon-skeleton synthesis. It requires actual fluid transfer, an alkalinity budget, and retention of the saturated intermediate.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Cyclic Strecker entry with alkaline hydration and acid amide cleavage", "description": "Hydroxide-promoted nitrile hydration is followed by discharge into an acidic geothermal pool for amide cleavage. This variant is available only if prolinamide survives transport and the receiving pool has adequate acidity; no spontaneous pH switching at an unchanged site is assumed.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Dialdehyde Strecker entry with nickel reduction and acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Conditional mono-Strecker capture and immediate ring closure precede Ni/H2 reduction. Only the saturated nitrile then encounters prolonged acidic hydrolysis. The pathway fails if Paal-Knorr diversion, cyanide poisoning, inadequate Ni activity, or nitrile over-reduction prevents useful prolinonitrile production.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_007", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Dialdehyde Strecker entry with nickel reduction and alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "After the same conditional nickel-mediated reduction as P5, saturated prolinonitrile enters an alkaline hydrothermal compartment separated from the hydrogenation surface. This retains the entry and catalyst uncertainties while replacing acidic hydrolysis with the alkaline mechanism.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Nickel-reduced entry with acid hydration and alkaline amide cleavage", "description": "Nickel reduction first removes the ring imine. Acid hydration then supplies the saturated amide for later alkaline cleavage. Transfer is confined to the saturated intermediate stage; the unreduced imine is neither stored nor transported between distant environments.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_007", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Dialdehyde Strecker entry with platinum reduction and acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Native Pt/H2 replaces nickel at the cyclic-imine reduction step, followed by acidic hydrolysis of the saturated nitrile and amide. This variant requires a rare, sufficiently exposed platinum surface and demonstrated resistance to poisoning under the actual feed composition.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Dialdehyde Strecker entry with platinum reduction and alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "Conditional Pt-mediated ring-imine reduction is followed by both alkaline hydrolysis stages. The saturated product is separated from platinum before heating, so the design does not intentionally expose the nitrile to simultaneous hydrogenation and hot hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_008", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Platinum-reduced entry with alkaline hydration and acid amide cleavage", "description": "The Pt-reduced nitrile undergoes alkaline hydration, followed by transfer of a saturated prolinamide-containing fluid into an acidic pool. This tenth graph path is a mixed-hydrolysis variant, not evidence for a tenth independently demonstrated prebiotic synthesis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_008", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_009", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_009", "rxn_010"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_010", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 511418.9221858978, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T07:20:08.991661", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Proline\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Ten graph-distinct, conditional route variants converge on racemic prolinonitrile and then prolinamide. Hydrolysis produces L-proline together with D-proline; no enantioenrichment or resolution is claimed. P1-P4 use cyclization of 4-aminobutanal followed by cyclic-imine Strecker addition, avoiding dialdehyde selectivity and catalytic hydrogenation problems. P5-P10 are less secure alternatives using succinaldehyde, Strecker addition, cyclization, and explicitly catalyst-dependent hydrogenation. In every productive route, the ring is saturated before nitrile or amide hydrolysis: no route assumes that an unreduced amino-aldehyde/imine pool survives prolonged hydrolysis. Acid-catalyzed and hydroxide-promoted hydrolysis provide alternative mechanisms; their combinations constitute route variants, not ten independent, experimentally validated prebiotic syntheses. Both C4 feedstocks are conditional inputs whose sustained geochemical supply remains unresolved. Accordingly, this is a complete graph for the declared conditional feeds, not a demonstrated closed synthesis from universally available inorganic feedstocks. Proposed conditions and residence times are screening windows, not measured optima or promised yields. A competing Paal-Knorr sink is included explicitly. Neutral-parent formulas represent compounds across protonation states; pH reservoirs and counterions are not expanded into separate speciation reactions. environment_formed records a representative formation environment when alternatives exist. D-proline and pyrrole are terminal coproduct/sink species assigned the schema's intermediate role because no coproduct role is available.\n\nMOLECULES (14 nodes):\n  - mol_001: C4H6O2 (succinaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: CHN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_005: H2 (hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_006: C5H8N2O (2-amino-5-oxopentanenitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C5H6N2 (3,4-dihydro-2H-pyrrole-2-carbonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C5H8N2 (racemic prolinonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C5H10N2O (racemic prolinamide) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C5H9NO2 (L-proline) [target]\n  - mol_011: C5H9NO2 (D-proline) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C4H9NO (gamma-aminobutyraldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_013: C4H7N (1-pyrroline) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C4H5N (pyrrole) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (11 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_006, mol_004 (Conditional mono-Strecker capture of succinaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_006 -> mol_007, mol_004 (Five-membered imine formation from the amino-aldehyde nitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_007, mol_005 -> mol_008 (Conditional Ni-catalyzed hydrogenation of the cyclic imine nitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_007, mol_005 -> mol_008 (Conditional Pt-catalyzed hydrogenation of the cyclic imine nitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_012 -> mol_013, mol_004 (Ring-chain equilibration of 4-aminobutanal to 1-pyrroline) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_013, mol_003 -> mol_008 (Cyclic-imine Strecker addition to form prolinonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_008, mol_004 -> mol_009 (Acid-catalyzed hydration of saturated prolinonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_008, mol_004 -> mol_009 (Hydroxide-promoted hydration of saturated prolinonitrile) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_009: mol_009, mol_004 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_002 (Acid hydrolysis of racemic prolinamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_009, mol_004 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_002 (Alkaline hydrolysis of racemic prolinamide) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_011: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_014, mol_004 (Competing Paal-Knorr diversion of succinaldehyde to pyrrole) [Surface]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Cyclic Strecker entry followed by acidic hydrolysis (4 steps)\n  - P2: Cyclic Strecker entry followed by alkaline hydrolysis (4 steps)\n  - P3: Cyclic Strecker entry with acid hydration and alkaline amide cleavage (4 steps)\n  - P4: Cyclic Strecker entry with alkaline hydration and acid amide cleavage (4 steps)\n  - P5: Dialdehyde Strecker entry with nickel reduction and acidic hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P6: Dialdehyde Strecker entry with nickel reduction and alkaline hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P7: Nickel-reduced entry with acid hydration and alkaline amide cleavage (5 steps)\n  - P8: Dialdehyde Strecker entry with platinum reduction and acidic hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P9: Dialdehyde Strecker entry with platinum reduction and alkaline hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P10: Platinum-reduced entry with alkaline hydration and acid amide cleavage (5 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 11 reactions | 8 balanced as written | 3 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_009 balanceable: 2 C5H10N2O + 2 H2O -> C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 2 NH3\n  - rxn_010 balanceable: 2 C5H10N2O + 2 H2O -> C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 2 NH3\n  - rxn_011 balanceable: C4H6O2 + NH3 -> C4H5N + 2 H2O\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"target_achievement\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"feedback\": \"<pathway-specific feedback>\",\n            \"approved\": <true if confidence >= 0.8>,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_ids with issues\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\"improvements\"]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"<id of best pathway>\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"<overall assessment>\"\n}\n\nNote: \"environmental_progression\" scores Environmental Consistency (conditions fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.86,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.86,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.9\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.88,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.68,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.89,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.83,\n      \"feedback\": \"The strongest conditional route: five-membered cyclic-imine formation, cyanide addition, and saturated aminonitrile hydrolysis provide a coherent route to racemic proline, including L-proline. Neither catalytic hydrogenation nor dialdehyde monofunctionalization is required. However, 'Surface' does not establish the acidity, temperature, or residence time needed for hydrolysis. Productive cyanide capture and hydrolysis must outcompete pyrroline oligomerization, imine hydrolysis, and possible retro-Strecker loss.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify concentrations, pH, temperature, water activity, and residence times for cyclic-imine capture and acidic hydrolysis.\",\n        \"Measure intermediate and proline yields, including competing losses and the L:D product ratio; enantioenrichment is not required to establish L-proline as a component.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.86,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.87\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.86,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.64,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.87,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.81,\n      \"feedback\": \"The cyclic Strecker entry followed by alkaline nitrile and amide hydrolysis is chemically coherent. Prolinamide can be a transient intermediate rather than an isolated product. The unresolved environmental questions are the actual hydrothermal temperature, pressure, alkalinity, and residence time, together with physically justified delivery of prolinonitrile. Product formation must exceed thermal degradation. Under the declared neutral-parent convention, alkaline prolinate formation is not a target-identity or balance error.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn