{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T07:23:46.574783", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: L-Proline\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.6\n- Feedback: This is a connected, sufficiently populated network with consistent pathway connectivity and credible formal routes to proline. The supplied deterministic check establishes no conservation violations: rxn_002 and rxn_020 require the reported racemate coefficients, and rxn_015 through rxn_017 require two protons. These small coefficients are not chemically suspicious. Reconcile the mol_022 formula/structure annotation by consistently representing one Fe atom with charge +2; this flag does not overturn the balance results. L-proline within a racemate satisfies the stated target, and the explicitly unexpanded mirror-image chemistry is not an isolated subgraph. The principal limitations are mechanistic and environmental: potentially competitive Paal-Knorr pyrrole formation during entry chemistry, competing reactions of the remaining aldehyde, unspecified catalytic activation for every H2 reduction, uncertain productive reduction by Fe(0), and uncertain survival of imine/amino-aldehyde pools during hydrolysis. General acidic, buffered, reducing, and shaded surface microenvironments are plausible, but the supplied entries do not establish operating temperatures, pH windows, H2 availability, active catalyst identities, residence times, or transfer with adequate intermediate retention. Succinaldehyde is accepted as a conditional starting input; its geochemical supply remains unestablished rather than being treated as a conservation defect. No research findings were supplied, so no separate literature-alignment penalty is applied. Route count, hub count, and environment order were not scoring criteria. P1 is the best conditional design, but the network requires substantial kinetic, selectivity, and environmental validation and does not meet the 0.9 network approval threshold.\n- Issues: ['This is the strongest conditional route: reducing the cyclic imine before hydrolysis limits exposure of the reactive imine/amino-aldehyde pool to hydrolytic conditions. Entry chemistry must nevertheless compete with pyrrole-forming Paal-Knorr chemistry, cyanohydrin formation, and other dialdehyde reactions. For rxn_008, H2 is a reductant but does not establish an effective hydrogenation without an active catalyst and suitable conditions. The final hydrolyses are chemically credible and would furnish L-proline within the acknowledged racemate.', 'The route is connected and chemically intelligible. rxn_009 can legitimately represent net hydration through an exchanging cyclic/open-chain pool; an intact cyclic imine need not persist throughout. The uncertainty is whether nitrile hydration preserves enough of that pool for subsequent reduction. rxn_010 also lacks an identified H2 catalyst and operating window. Prolinamide hydrolysis is a credible final transformation, but does not resolve the upstream kinetic and selectivity gaps.', 'Complete hydrolysis before reduction gives a valid formal route through pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid. However, nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis must proceed while the imine/amino-aldehyde pool remains recoverable. Acid-induced ring opening is not itself a failure, but irreversible decomposition and inadequate hydrolysis rates remain unresolved. The final imine reduction is chemically plausible with suitable catalysis; the supplied record does not establish that H2 can accomplish it in the stated setting.', 'Hydration followed by five-membered-ring condensation is structurally coherent. The principal additional uncertainty is selective nitrile hydration while retaining the reactive amino-aldehyde skeleton. Ring-chain equilibration is allowed and is not itself a defect in this pathway. Once the cyclic amide is available, reduction and prolinamide hydrolysis provide a sensible route to the target, but the H2 reduction remains insufficiently specified.', 'All intermediates connect correctly to proline, but the route depends on successful nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis before the reactive imine is reduced. The network does not establish a hydrolysis window that combines useful rates with preservation of the amino-aldehyde/cyclic-imine pool. Subsequent H2 reduction requires catalytic activation that is not identified. These are kinetic and environmental uncertainties, not atom-balance violations.', 'Glutamate-semialdehyde cyclization to pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid is a particularly well-founded transformation. The difficult part is delivering intact semialdehyde through nitrile and amide hydrolysis rather than assuming that conditions adequate for hydrolysis preserve the reactive carbonyl-containing substrate. Allowing cyclic/open-chain exchange removes any requirement to isolate each open-chain intermediate, but does not establish survival or productive rates. Final H2 reduction remains catalyst-dependent.', 'The iron reduction is atom- and charge-balanced with two protons, as reported by the deterministic check. That establishes electron bookkeeping, not productive imine reduction. Native iron is a stated input, but its surface activity, corrosion rate, passivation, and selectivity toward the imine rather than competing processes are unspecified. If prolinonitrile is obtained, the remaining hydrolyses provide a credible route to the target.', 'This evaluation reaches the cyclic amide through cyclization followed by rxn_009. Hydration through an exchanging ring-chain pool is plausible, but substrate recovery is unestablished. The balanced Fe(0) reduction then requires evidence that useful reduction competes successfully with corrosion and surface deactivation. Prolinamide hydrolysis is chemically sound, but productive delivery of prolinamide under the proposed conditions remains uncertain.', 'Consistent with the pathway description, this evaluation follows hydrolysis through glutamate semialdehyde and then rxn_007. The ring closure is strong chemistry, but recovery of its precursor through both hydrolysis stages is uncertain. The final iron-mediated reduction is correctly balanced yet lacks a demonstrated productive mechanism or operating window. Acidic conditions that expose fresh iron can also promote corrosion, so substrate reduction cannot be inferred from iron dissolution alone.', 'Cyclization of gamma-aminobutyraldehyde and cyclic Strecker addition provide a credible route to racemic prolinonitrile. The bottleneck is rxn_018: reduction must favor the monoimine over the remaining aldehyde, and the resulting pyrroline must escape further reduction to pyrrolidine before cyanide trapping. No catalyst or kinetic staging establishes that selectivity. The final hydrolyses can furnish the stated target without requiring enantioenrichment.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.79\n- Feedback: The network has strong graph validity: sufficient molecule and reaction nodes, consistent IDs, a connected productive network, and an attached competing sink. The deterministic check establishes that all 11 reactions conserve atoms and charge with appropriate coefficients; none is a conservation violation. Explicitly write 2 mol_009 + 2 mol_004 -> mol_010 + mol_011 + 2 mol_002 for rxn_009 and rxn_010, and two product waters for rxn_011. The neutral-parent protonation convention is acceptable. Chemically, the cyclic-imine Strecker core is credible, and saturating the ring before prolonged hydrolysis removes an important vulnerability of unreduced intermediates. Racemic production does include L-proline, but does not establish enantiopure L-proline. The declared conditional C4 feeds are accepted as the evaluation scope; failure to synthesize them from inorganic inputs is not a graph-validity defect. Their environmental supply nevertheless remains unresolved. The principal limitations are unspecified quantitative conditions, unverified reaction branching, catalyst availability and chemoselectivity for rxn_003/rxn_004, and missing transport and buffering models. The Paal-Knorr sink rxn_011 is chemically plausible and appropriately included, but its inclusion does not quantify the productive flux competing with it. No supplied studies, yields, or kinetic measurements validate the exact connected implementation. The alternative routes do not provide independent experimental corroboration. Listed problematic reactions indicate these chemical or environmental uncertainties, not balance failures. Overall, this is a plausible conditional synthesis network with a stronger cyclic-Strecker core and substantially less secure dialdehyde branches, but it is not approved at the network threshold of 0.9.\n- Issues: ['Acidic nitrile hydration followed by alkaline amide cleavage is a valid chemical sequence. Its implementation requires an appreciable prolinamide flux into an alkaline setting and enough buffering capacity to maintain alkaline hydrolysis after acidic fluid arrives. The concern is unspecified speciation, mixing, and reaction timing, not the order or number of environments. Continued acidic hydrolysis could also produce proline, but would not validate this particular hybrid sequence.', 'The transformations are individually plausible, but this variant requires prolinamide to leave alkaline hydrothermal conditions before extensive further hydrolysis or degradation, followed by effective acidic cleavage. No kinetic or transport evidence establishes that intermediate handoff. Conversion directly to proline in the alkaline stage would still support target production, but not the specified acid-finished pathway. The racemic outcome is consistent with formation of L-proline as one component.', 'The sequence is atom-balanced and structurally coherent, but productive flux depends on two unverified selectivity gates. Succinaldehyde must undergo mono-Strecker capture and cyclization rather than pyrrole formation, further Strecker reaction, or oligomerization. Nickel must then reduce the cyclic imine while preserving the nitrile. Catalyst phase, hydrogen activity, and resistance to residual cyanide or environmental poisons are unspecified. The downstream acidic hydrolysis chemistry is substantially stronger than the entry-stage evidence.', 'Alkaline hydrolysis provides a credible finish once prolinonitrile exists, but does not resolve uncertain dialdehyde selectivity or nickel-catalyzed chemoselectivity. The catalyst and hydrogen supply need environmental justification, and the alkaline hydrothermal stage needs a defined operating window and transport mechanism. Formation of the saturated ring before hydrolysis is a sound feature, although it does not establish survival at arbitrary hydrothermal temperatures.', 'This is a valid conditional sequence, but it inherits the uncertain succinaldehyde branching and selective nickel reduction. The acid-to-base hydrolysis handoff additionally requires a demonstrated prolinamide flux and adequate downstream alkalinity. There is no inherent chemical prohibition against this environmental sequence; its weakness is the absence of catalyst-specific evidence, reaction timing, and fluid-composition constraints.', 'Replacing nickel with platinum does not by itself establish selective imine hydrogenation or environmental availability. An active platinum-bearing material, sufficient hydrogen activity, and preservation of the nitrile must be demonstrated. Mono-Strecker selectivity remains an upstream uncertainty despite the explicitly included pyrrole sink. Acidic hydrolysis is plausible once saturated prolinonitrile forms. No supplied comparative evidence justifies ranking this catalyst alternative above or below P5.', 'The alkaline finish is chemically reasonable, but the complete route remains conditional on productive dialdehyde capture and nitrile-preserving platinum hydrogenation. Neither the active catalyst environment nor the hydrothermal operating conditions are established. The final hydrolysis transformations can generate L-proline within a racemate; the reduced target score reflects uncertain upstream delivery and product survival, not a requirement for enantiopure material.', 'The covalent sequence is coherent, but productive implementation requires selective dialdehyde capture, selective platinum reduction, and export of a transient prolinamide pool from alkaline hydrothermal conditions into an effective acidic hydrolysis setting. Those kinetic and environmental requirements are not demonstrated. These are implementation uncertainties rather than conservation violations or an objection to the environmental ordering.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_mol