,\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Gamma-aminobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3,4-dihydro-2H-pyrrole\",\n      \"common_name\": \"1-Pyrroline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Prolinonitrile, racemic pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHNO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Isocyanic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Isocyanic acid; neutral parent of cyanate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHNO/c2-1-3/h2H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H9N3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-cyanopyrrolidine-1-carboxamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"N-Carbamoylprolinonitrile, racemic pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H9N3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-imino-1,3-diazabicyclo[3.3.0]octan-2-one\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Proline-derived iminohydantoin, racemic pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-cyanopyrrolidine-1-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Prolinonitrile N-carbamic acid, racemic pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-imino-3-oxa-1-azabicyclo[3.3.0]octan-2-one\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Proline-derived iminooxazolidinone, racemic pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1,3-diazabicyclo[3.3.0]octane-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Proline hydantoin, racemic pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Prolinamide, racemic pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H11N3O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Pyrrolidine-1,2-dicarboxamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"N-Carbamoylprolinamide, racemic pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H10N2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1-carbamoylpyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"N-Carbamoylproline, racemic pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-Proline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2R)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D-Proline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m1/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Intramolecular condensation of 4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, UV-shielded shallow pores; proposed pH 7.5-8.5 and total C4 substrate concentration 1-5 mM. Treat aldehyde, hydrate, and cyclic imine as an exchanging pool rather than isolated compounds.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular amine addition to the aldehyde gives a five-membered hemiaminal, followed by reversible dehydration to 1-pyrroline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Five-membered cyclic-imine formation is chemically well grounded and eliminates the competing two-carbonyl Paal-Knorr entry chemistry of succinaldehyde. No reduction catalyst is needed. Oligomerization and imine hydrolysis remain possible. The exact pool composition and geochemical supply of intact 4-aminobutanal are unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclic-imine Strecker capture\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same compartment as rxn_001; proposed pH 7.5-8.5, total cyanide 5-20 mM, and C4 substrate 1-5 mM. Screen over 10 minutes to 24 hours in shaded, water-retaining pores. These are test conditions, not measured conversion times.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the electrophilic carbon of the protonated cyclic imine; proton transfer gives saturated pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a cyclic application of Strecker aminonitrile chemistry. It supplies the carboxyl-carbon precursor while saturating the ring without H2, native metal, or a separate hydrogenation. Achiral capture produces both configurations. Productive trapping must be measured against imine hydrolysis, cyanide loss, oligomerization, and retro-Strecker reaction; equilibrium alone does not establish useful yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanate carbamoylation of prolinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"315-345 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 7.5-8.5; prolinonitrile 1-5 mM and total cyanate 5-50 mM. Isocyanic acid is represented by its neutral parent but is predominantly cyanate at this pH. Examine hours-to-days exposure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The secondary amine attacks the electrophilic carbon of isocyanic acid, forming an N-substituted urea.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amine carbamoylation by cyanate has established reaction-class precedent. Application to this prolinonitrile pool under the stated environmental concentrations is conditional, not demonstrated here. Cyanate hydrolysis and carbamoylation of competing amines can consume the reagent. Concentrated cyanate delivery is an additional feedstock requirement, not an assumed universal environmental feature.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclization of N-carbamoylprolinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Carbonate/bicarbonate buffer for general acid-base catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Carbonate/bicarbonate buffer\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 8-9 and buffer concentration 20-100 mM; shaded geothermal pool. Screen for cyclization over hours to days without assuming quantitative conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal ureido nitrogen attacks the pendant nitrile intramolecularly, followed by proton transfer to form a fused five-membered iminohydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Intramolecular ureidonitrile cyclization provides a chemically coherent alternative to direct nitrile hydration. Buffer is assigned only to proton-transfer catalysis, not as an unsubstantiated mineral template. The fused proline substrate's rate, equilibrium, and competing hydrolysis are unverified; this branch is speculative at the connected prebiotic-system level.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible N-carboxylation of prolinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed carbonate-rich pore water at pH 8-9, total inorganic carbon 50-200 mM, and prolinonitrile 1-5 mM. Dissolved CO2 must be calculated from pH and gas exchange rather than equated with total carbonate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible addition of the secondary amine to dissolved CO2 forms the N-carbamate pool, represented as its neutral carbamic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Secondary-amine carbamate formation is established chemistry. Its equilibrium abundance may nevertheless be low for this substrate in dilute water. This node is included because carbamate formation is a mechanistic requirement of the proposed Bucherer-Bergs-type branch, not because carbonate availability guarantees hydantoin synthesis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbamate cyclization to an iminooxazolidinone\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Carbonate/bicarbonate buffer for general acid-base catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Carbonate/bicarbonate buffer\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same carbonate-rich compartment as rxn_005; proposed pH 8-9. Maintain inorganic-carbon activity and assay the competing return to free aminonitrile.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbamate oxygen attacks the adjacent nitrile carbon intramolecularly, producing a fused iminooxazolidinone after proton transfer.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This follows the commonly proposed carbamate-cyclization logic of Bucherer-Bergs chemistry. General reaction-class precedent does not validate this fused secondary-aminonitrile substrate. Decarboxylation and hydrolysis can prevent accumulation; productive flux through this specific intermediate remains speculative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Iminooxazolidinone rearrangement to proline hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Carbonate/bicarbonate buffer for proton-transfer catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Carbonate/bicarbonate buffer\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same compartment as rxn_006; proposed pH 8-9. Follow hydantoin formation and competing hydrolysis over hours to days.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proton transfer and acyl rearrangement replace the endocyclic oxygen linkage with the imino nitrogen linkage, yielding the hydantoin constitutional isomer.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a mechanistic completion proposed by analogy with Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin formation. No atoms are added or removed. Applicability to this fused ring and the stated dilute aqueous conditions is unverified; it must not be treated as an experimentally established proline source.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic hydration of prolinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"365-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed acid-finishing envelope: pH 0-1 in an anoxic, shaded, chloride-rich geothermal pool; screen total exposure from 1 to 30 days. Volcanic acid input must overcome the measured initial alkalinity. Continue directly to rxn_009 without isolating the amide.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation, water addition, and imidic-acid tautomerization produce prolinamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acidic aminonitrile hydration is a credible transformation class. The saturated ring avoids relying on survival of a cyclic imine throughout hydrolysis. The proposed temperature and acidity do not establish a rate or yield; retro-Strecker loss and substrate recovery must be measured. Prolinamide is an in-compartment intermediate, not an assumed transportable harvest.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic prolinamide hydrolysis to racemic proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"365-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same pH 0-1 compartment and uninterrupted exposure as rxn_008. Acid capacity must accommodate ammonia production and any carried-over buffer. Cool and dilute only after the desired endpoint.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed amide hydrolysis releases the carboxylic acid and ammonia, present mainly as ammonium under these conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is established amide-hydrolysis chemistry, although a useful environmental residence time remains to be demonstrated. Racemate bookkeeping is explicitly 2 mol_013 + 2 mol_002 -> mol_017 + mol_018 + 2 mol_016. No enantiomeric enrichment is implied.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic iminohydantoin hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"365-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Use the same pH 0-1 acid-finishing compartment as the subsequent hydantoin hydrolysis. Screen within a total 1-30 day exposure; do not presume the imino intermediate survives transfer quantitatively.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water addition to the protonated exocyclic imino group and ammonia elimination replace C=NH with C=O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amidine-to-carbonyl hydrolysis is chemically coherent and avoids any hydrogenation requirement. Exact kinetics for this fused iminohydantoin are unavailable here. Ring cleavage and other hydrolytic products must be included in experimental carbon recovery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic hydration of N-carbamoylprolinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"365-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 0-1 acid-finishing compartment; total exposure 1-30 days. The resulting amide remains in place for rxn_012 or rxn_013.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed nitrile hydration forms the carboxamide while retaining the N-carbamoyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile hydration is credible, but selective retention of the ureido group is a substrate-specific uncertainty. Competing decarbamoylation could return material to the direct prolinonitrile route rather than validate this branch. Neither quantitative amide accumulation nor isolation is assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic hydrolysis of N-carbamoylprolinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"365-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same pH 0-1 compartment as rxn_011 and rxn_015; no intermediate export or pH reversal. Measure branching against rxn_013.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the alpha-carboxamide releases ammonia and gives N-carbamoylproline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ordinary carboxamide hydrolysis supplies a chemically direct route to the protected amino acid. Selectivity over ureido-group cleavage and hydantoin formation is unknown. This is a proposed competing channel, not a claim that the protected acid will accumulate substantially.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-assisted cyclodeamination of N-carbamoylprolinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"365-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same pH 0-1 compartment as rxn_011. Evaluate as a parallel channel rather than assuming a separate, selectively harvested reaction stage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular acyl substitution by the terminal ureido nitrogen closes the hydantoin ring with ammonia elimination.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Five-membered cyclization is structurally coherent, but amide substitution in water competes with simple hydrolysis and the ureido nitrogen is weakly nucleophilic. Productive cyclodeamination of this exact substrate under this envelope is speculative. P7 is valid only if a measurable hydantoin-forming branch is demonstrated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic opening of proline hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"365-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same pH 0-1 acid-finishing compartment; assess within a total 1-30 day exposure. N-Carbamoylproline is not removed before rxn_015.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the hydantoin acyl-nitrogen bond opens the added ring to N-carbamoylproline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis to a ureido acid has established reaction-class precedent. Acidic opening can be slow and reversible, particularly for a fused system. The stated envelope is a test of accessibility, not evidence that a useful rate exceeds reclosure or decomposition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic decarbamoylation to racemic proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"365-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same pH 0-1 acid-finishing compartment. Monitor residual protected acid, hydantoin reformation, and proline recovery; do not prolong heating indefinitely if release is too slow.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the N-carbamoyl group releases proline; the one-carbon nitrogenous fragment decomposes to carbon dioxide and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the principal finishing bottleneck of the acidic protected routes: urea-group cleavage may require conditions harsher than those compatible with useful environmental recovery. The proposed window is unvalidated. Explicit racemate balance: 2 mol_015 + 2 mol_002 -> mol_017 + mol_018 + 2 mol_016 + 2 mol_009.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline hydration of prolinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"415-435 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar; liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed in-situ pH 10-11 in warm alkaline rock pores, not a black-smoker core. Screen 1 hour to 10 days. Measure substrate concentration after delivery and dilution; continue directly to rxn_017 in the same fluid.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide addition to the nitrile, proton transfer, and tautomerization yield prolinamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Base-promoted nitrile hydration is a real alternative mechanism to acidic hydration. No mineral catalyst or hydrogen supply is assumed. Useful flux at the proposed alkalinity is not established, and thermal degradation can compete. Buffer capacity and in-situ pH must be measured rather than inferred from room-temperature vent-fluid values.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline prolinamide hydrolysis to racemic proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"415-435 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar; liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same in-situ pH 10-11 compartment as rxn_016. Include conversion to carboxylate in the alkalinity budget. Cool the entire reacted fluid after the selected endpoint; no acid-finished amide handoff is required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide-mediated amide cleavage gives prolinate and ammonia; neutral-parent products are used for graph bookkeeping.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis is chemically credible, but the competition between its rate and high-temperature substrate loss requires measurement. Explicit balance: 2 mol_013 + 2 mol_002 -> mol_017 + mol_018 + 2 mol_016. No preferential formation or preservation of L-proline is assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline iminohydantoin hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"415-435 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar; liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed in-situ pH 10-11; same compartment as rxn_022 and rxn_023. Screen within a total 1 hour to 10 day exposure and determine iminohydantoin recovery after delivery.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water/hydroxide addition and proton-transfer-assisted ammonia elimination convert the exocyclic imino group into a carbonyl.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a plausible hydrolytic transformation, not a metal reduction. Amidines can resist alkaline hydrolysis, so the productive rate of this fused substrate is a specific uncertainty. Higher temperature cannot be assumed to solve that problem without increasing competing decomposition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline hydration of N-carbamoylprolinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"415-435 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar; liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed in-situ pH 10-11; same compartment as rxn_020 or rxn_021. Measure the inlet organic concentration after mixing, and screen total exposure from 1 hour to 10 days.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-promoted nitrile hydration produces N-carbamoylprolinamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The nitrile-hydration class is credible, but retention of the N-carbamoyl group is conditional. Cyclization, decarbamoylation, and degradation can compete. Subsequent reactions occur in the same fluid, so the pathway does not rely on exporting a transient amide before further hydrolysis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline hydrolysis of N-carbamoylprolinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"415-435 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar; liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same in-situ pH 10-11 compartment as rxn_019 and rxn_023; no intervening acidification. Include carboxylate formation in the fluid's alkalinity balance.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide-mediated carboxamide cleavage releases ammonia and produces N-carbamoylprolinate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"A terminal carboxamide can hydrolyze without requiring reduction or cleavage of the pyrrolidine ring. Relative rates of carboxamide hydrolysis, ureido-group cleavage, and thermal loss remain unmeasured. This node represents a possible channel rather than guaranteed selective accumulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Thermal cyclodeamination of N-carbamoylprolinamide in alkaline fluid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"415-435 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar; liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same in-situ pH 10-11 compartment as rxn_019. Treat this as a potentially minor competing channel, not an independently optimized or selectively isolated stage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular attack of the terminal ureido nitrogen on the carboxamide, with proton transfer and ammonia loss, would close the hydantoin ring.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the weakest enumerated channel. Five-membered closure is structurally possible, but amide leaving-group chemistry is unfavorable and alkaline hydrolysis may dominate completely. No effective mineral catalyst is asserted. Productive cyclodeamination under these conditions is speculative; P8 must be rejected if kinetic testing finds negligible hydantoin flux.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_022\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline opening of proline hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"415-435 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar; liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed in-situ pH 10-11; continue to rxn_023 in the same compartment. Screen within a total 1 hour to 10 day exposure and measure both remaining hydantoin and ring-opened material.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide attacks the hydantoin carbonyl adjacent to the alpha carbon; acyl-nitrogen cleavage opens the added ring to N-carbamoylprolinate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Base-promoted hydantoin ring opening is a well-founded reaction class and is mechanistically distinct from acidic opening. Nevertheless, useful rates for this fused substrate at geochemically available alkalinity are not established. No enzymatic hydantoinase activity is assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_023\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline thermal decarbamoylation to racemic proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"415-435 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar; liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same in-situ pH 10-11 compartment as the preceding alkaline reactions. CO2 is mainly bicarbonate/carbonate here. Quantify residual N-carbamoylproline and proline survival before cooling; do not presume complete deprotection.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the N-carbamoyl group releases prolinate, ammonia, and inorganic carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the principal bottleneck of the alkaline protected routes. Nonenzymatic ureido-group removal can be much slower than hydantoin opening, and the proposed window may fail before useful proline release occurs. It is a conditional finishing hypothesis, not a demonstrated environmental conversion. Explicit neutral-parent balance: 2 mol_015 + 2 mol_002 -> mol_017 + mol_018 + 2 mol_016 + 2 mol_009.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct cyclic Strecker route with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The strongest conditional sequence: ring-chain equilibration and cyanide capture are followed by uninterrupted acidic nitrile and amide hydrolysis. No dialdehyde, hydrogenation, or amide transfer is required. L-Proline is one component of the racemic product.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct cyclic Strecker route with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The same credible aminonitrile entry is followed by a wholly alkaline hydrothermal finish. This is a distinct hydrolysis mechanism, not independent evidence for the entry reaction. Dilution, alkalinity, and thermal recovery remain limiting tests.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanate capture and iminohydantoin formation with acidic finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"Carbamoylation precedes intramolecular nitrile capture. The resulting iminohydantoin undergoes a wholly acidic hydrolysis sequence. Cyclization and final decarbamoylation are conditional bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanate capture and iminohydantoin formation with alkaline finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"The iminohydantoin branch is completed in one alkaline hydrothermal compartment. Useful imino-group hydrolysis and nonenzymatic decarbamoylation must both be demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_022\", \"rxn_023\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Open-chain ureido-substituent hydrolysis with acidic finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"N-Carbamoylprolinonitrile is hydrated without first forming the additional hydantoin ring. Carboxamide hydrolysis and decarbamoylation then proceed in the same acidic compartment. The pyrrolidine ring remains intact throughout the proposed productive channel.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Open-chain ureido-substituent hydrolysis with alkaline finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"The corresponding protected nitrile-to-amide-to-acid sequence occurs entirely within one alkaline hydrothermal finishing compartment. No transient amide export is assumed; final ureido-group removal is unvalidated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_023\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration-before-hydantoin cyclization with acidic finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"After carbamoylation, nitrile hydration precedes the proposed cyclodeamination of the resulting carboxamide. This is a chemically distinct but speculative ordering; simple amide hydrolysis may outcompete hydantoin formation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration-before-hydantoin cyclization with alkaline finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"A formal alkaline counterpart to P7. This is the lowest-confidence enumerated pathway because cyclodeamination may have negligible flux relative to hydrolysis. It is explicitly a testable candidate, not an established prebiotic synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_021\", \"rxn_022\", \"rxn_023\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-type carbonate branch with acidic finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"CO2 capture replaces the cyanate-carbamoylation entry to the additional ring. Proposed carbamate cyclization and rearrangement supply proline hydantoin, followed by wholly acidic finishing. The fused-substrate implementation remains speculative.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-type carbonate branch with alkaline finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"The carbonate-derived hydantoin branch is followed by ring opening and attempted decarbamoylation in one alkaline hydrothermal compartment. Carbamate trapping, delivery without excessive dilution, and final proline release are separate unresolved requirements.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_022\", \"rxn_023\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_015\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_022\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_022\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_023\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Proline", "overall_strategy": "An evidence-limited, connected candidate network centered on cyclic-imine Strecker chemistry. Conditional delivery of 4-aminobutanal supplies an equilibrating 1-pyrroline pool; cyanide capture forms saturated, racemic prolinonitrile before prolonged hydrolysis. This avoids succinaldehyde/Paal-Knorr competition and requires neither unactivated H2 nor speculative Fe(0) reduction. Ten distinct reaction sequences are enumerated: five covalent sequences, each with either an entirely acidic or an entirely alkaline hydrolysis module. These are not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses. Direct prolinonitrile hydrolysis is the strongest conditional route; cyanate-derived and Bucherer-Bergs-type hydantoin branches are less secure, particularly their substrate-specific cyclization and deprotection kinetics. All stereogenic chemistry is achiral, so L-proline is obtained together with D-proline, not enantiopure. The environmental supply of 4-aminobutanal and concentrated cyanate remains unestablished. Numerical conditions below are proposed screening envelopes, not experimentally established productive windows. Acidic modules operate in one acidified surface compartment; alkaline modules operate in one warm alkaline hydrothermal compartment. No pathway requires harvesting prolinamide between acidic and alkaline hydrolysis stages. Productive operation still requires measurements of branching, carbon recovery, residence time, dilution, and buffering capacity. Formulae use neutral-parent bookkeeping; actual protonation, carbonate, cyanate, and ammonia speciation must be calculated at the stated pH. Unspecified stereochemistry denotes racemic precursor pools. environment_formed records a primary formation setting; alternative settings are specified in reaction nodes. Non-target terminal coproducts use the schema's intermediate role.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C4H9NO", "iupac_name": "4-aminobutanal", "common_name": "Gamma-aminobutyraldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "C4H7N", "iupac_name": "3,4-dihydro-2H-pyrrole", "common_name": "1-Pyrroline", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C5H8N2", "iupac_name": "Pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile", "common_name": "Prolinonitrile, racemic pool", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CHNO", "iupac_name": "Isocyanic acid", "common_name": "Isocyanic acid; neutral parent of cyanate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHNO/c2-1-3/h2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C6H9N3O", "iupac_name": "2-cyanopyrrolidine-1-carboxamide", "common_name": "N-Carbamoylprolinonitrile, racemic pool", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C6H9N3O", "iupac_name": "4-imino-1,3-diazabicyclo[3.3.0]octan-2-one", "common_name": "Proline-derived iminohydantoin, racemic pool", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "Carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C6H8N2O2", "iupac_name": "2-cyanopyrrolidine-1-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "Prolinonitrile N-carbamic acid, racemic pool", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C6H8N2O2", "iupac_name": "4-imino-3-oxa-1-azabicyclo[3.3.0]octan-2-one", "common_name": "Proline-derived iminooxazolidinone, racemic pool", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C6H8N2O2", "iupac_name": "1,3-diazabicyclo[3.3.0]octane-2,4-dione", "common_name": "Proline hydantoin, racemic pool", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C5H10N2O", "iupac_name": "Pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide", "common_name": "Prolinamide, racemic pool", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C6H11N3O2", "iupac_name": "Pyrrolidine-1,2-dicarboxamide", "common_name": "N-Carbamoylprolinamide, racemic pool", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C6H10N2O3", "iupac_name": "1-carbamoylpyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "N-Carbamoylproline, racemic pool", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "Ammonia", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "L-Proline", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "D-Proline", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m1/s1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Intramolecular condensation of 4-aminobutanal", "inputs": ["mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_003", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, UV-shielded shallow pores; proposed pH 7.5-8.5 and total C4 substrate concentration 1-5 mM. Treat aldehyde, hydrate, and cyclic imine as an exchanging pool rather than isolated compounds."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular amine addition to the aldehyde gives a five-membered hemiaminal, followed by reversible dehydration to 1-pyrroline.", "reasoning": "Five-membered cyclic-imine formation is chemically well grounded and eliminates the competing two-carbonyl Paal-Knorr entry chemistry of succinaldehyde. No reduction catalyst is needed. Oligomerization and imine hydrolysis remain possible. The exact pool composition and geochemical supply of intact 4-aminobutanal are unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyclic-imine Strecker capture", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Same compartment as rxn_001; proposed pH 7.5-8.5, total cyanide 5-20 mM, and C4 substrate 1-5 mM. Screen over 10 minutes to 24 hours in shaded, water-retaining pores. These are test conditions, not measured conversion times."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the electrophilic carbon of the protonated cyclic imine; proton transfer gives saturated pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is a cyclic application of Strecker aminonitrile chemistry. It supplies the carboxyl-carbon precursor while saturating the ring without H2, native metal, or a separate hydrogenation. Achiral capture produces both configurations. Productive trapping must be measured against imine hydrolysis, cyanide loss, oligomerization, and retro-Strecker reaction; equilibrium alone does not establish useful yield."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cyanate carbamoylation of prolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "315-345 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 7.5-8.5; prolinonitrile 1-5 mM and total cyanate 5-50 mM. Isocyanic acid is represented by its neutral parent but is predominantly cyanate at this pH. Examine hours-to-days exposure."}, "mechanism": "The secondary amine attacks the electrophilic carbon of isocyanic acid, forming an N-substituted urea.", "reasoning": "Amine carbamoylation by cyanate has established reaction-class precedent. Application to this prolinonitrile pool under the stated environmental concentrations is conditional, not demonstrated here. Cyanate hydrolysis and carbamoylation of competing amines can consume the reagent. Concentrated cyanate delivery is an additional feedstock requirement, not an assumed universal environmental feature."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Cyclization of N-carbamoylprolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Carbonate/bicarbonate buffer for general acid-base catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Carbonate/bicarbonate buffer", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8-9 and buffer concentration 20-100 mM; shaded geothermal pool. Screen for cyclization over hours to days without assuming quantitative conversion."}, "mechanism": "The terminal ureido nitrogen attacks the pendant nitrile intramolecularly, followed by proton transfer to form a fused five-membered iminohydantoin.", "reasoning": "Intramolecular ureidonitrile cyclization provides a chemically coherent alternative to direct nitrile hydration. Buffer is assigned only to proton-transfer catalysis, not as an unsubstantiated mineral template. The fused proline substrate's rate, equilibrium, and competing hydrolysis are unverified; this branch is speculative at the connected prebiotic-system level."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Reversible N-carboxylation of prolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-340 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed carbonate-rich pore water at pH 8-9, total inorganic carbon 50-200 mM, and prolinonitrile 1-5 mM. Dissolved CO2 must be calculated from pH and gas exchange rather than equated with total carbonate."}, "mechanism": "Reversible addition of the secondary amine to dissolved CO2 forms the N-carbamate pool, represented as its neutral carbamic acid.", "reasoning": "Secondary-amine carbamate formation is established chemistry. Its equilibrium abundance may nevertheless be low for this substrate in dilute water. This node is included because carbamate formation is a mechanistic requirement of the proposed Bucherer-Bergs-type branch, not because carbonate availability guarantees hydantoin synthesis."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Carbamate cyclization to an iminooxazolidinone", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Carbonate/bicarbonate buffer for general acid-base catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Carbonate/bicarbonate buffer", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same carbonate-rich compartment as rxn_005; proposed pH 8-9. Maintain inorganic-carbon activity and assay the competing return to free aminonitrile."}, "mechanism": "Carbamate oxygen attacks the adjacent nitrile carbon intramolecularly, producing a fused iminooxazolidinone after proton transfer.", "reasoning": "This follows the commonly proposed carbamate-cyclization logic of Bucherer-Bergs chemistry. General reaction-class precedent does not validate this fused secondary-aminonitrile substrate. Decarboxylation and hydrolysis can prevent accumulation; productive flux through this specific intermediate remains speculative."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Iminooxazolidinone rearrangement to proline hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Carbonate/bicarbonate buffer for proton-transfer catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Carbonate/bicarbonate buffer", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same compartment as rxn_006; proposed pH 8-9. Follow hydantoin formation and competing hydrolysis over hours to days."}, "mechanism": "Proton transfer and acyl rearrangement replace the endocyclic oxygen linkage with the imino nitrogen linkage, yielding the hydantoin constitutional isomer.", "reasoning": "This is a mechanistic completion proposed by analogy with Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin formation. No atoms are added or removed. Applicability to this fused ring and the stated dilute aqueous conditions is unverified; it must not be treated as an experimentally established proline source."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Acidic hydration of prolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium"], "conditions": {"temperature": "365-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed acid-finishing envelope: pH 0-1 in an anoxic, shaded, chloride-rich geothermal pool; screen total exposure from 1 to 30 days. Volcanic acid input must overcome the measured initial alkalinity. Continue directly to rxn_009 without isolating the amide."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation, water addition, and imidic-acid tautomerization produce prolinamide.", "reasoning": "Acidic aminonitrile hydration is a credible transformation class. The saturated ring avoids relying on survival of a cyclic imine throughout hydrolysis. The proposed temperature and acidity do not establish a rate or yield; retro-Strecker loss and substrate recovery must be measured. Prolinamide is an in-compartment intermediate, not an assumed transportable harvest."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Acidic prolinamide hydrolysis to racemic proline", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium"], "conditions": {"temperature": "365-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same pH 0-1 compartment and uninterrupted exposure as rxn_008. Acid capacity must accommodate ammonia production and any carried-over buffer. Cool and dilute only after the desired endpoint."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed amide hydrolysis releases the carboxylic acid and ammonia, present mainly as ammonium under these conditions.", "reasoni