cumulation nor isolation is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Acidic hydrolysis of N-carbamoylprolinamide", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium"], "conditions": {"temperature": "365-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same pH 0-1 compartment as rxn_011 and rxn_015; no intermediate export or pH reversal. Measure branching against rxn_013."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of the alpha-carboxamide releases ammonia and gives N-carbamoylproline.", "reasoning": "Ordinary carboxamide hydrolysis supplies a chemically direct route to the protected amino acid. Selectivity over ureido-group cleavage and hydantoin formation is unknown. This is a proposed competing channel, not a claim that the protected acid will accumulate substantially."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Acid-assisted cyclodeamination of N-carbamoylprolinamide", "inputs": ["mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium"], "conditions": {"temperature": "365-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same pH 0-1 compartment as rxn_011. Evaluate as a parallel channel rather than assuming a separate, selectively harvested reaction stage."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular acyl substitution by the terminal ureido nitrogen closes the hydantoin ring with ammonia elimination.", "reasoning": "Five-membered cyclization is structurally coherent, but amide substitution in water competes with simple hydrolysis and the ureido nitrogen is weakly nucleophilic. Productive cyclodeamination of this exact substrate under this envelope is speculative. P7 is valid only if a measurable hydantoin-forming branch is demonstrated."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Acidic opening of proline hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium"], "conditions": {"temperature": "365-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same pH 0-1 acid-finishing compartment; assess within a total 1-30 day exposure. N-Carbamoylproline is not removed before rxn_015."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the hydantoin acyl-nitrogen bond opens the added ring to N-carbamoylproline.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin hydrolysis to a ureido acid has established reaction-class precedent. Acidic opening can be slow and reversible, particularly for a fused system. The stated envelope is a test of accessibility, not evidence that a useful rate exceeds reclosure or decomposition."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Acidic decarbamoylation to racemic proline", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_016", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium"], "conditions": {"temperature": "365-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same pH 0-1 acid-finishing compartment. Monitor residual protected acid, hydantoin reformation, and proline recovery; do not prolong heating indefinitely if release is too slow."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the N-carbamoyl group releases proline; the one-carbon nitrogenous fragment decomposes to carbon dioxide and ammonia.", "reasoning": "This is the principal finishing bottleneck of the acidic protected routes: urea-group cleavage may require conditions harsher than those compatible with useful environmental recovery. The proposed window is unvalidated. Explicit racemate balance: 2 mol_015 + 2 mol_002 -> mol_017 + mol_018 + 2 mol_016 + 2 mol_009."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Alkaline hydration of prolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "415-435 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar; liquid water", "catalyst": "Hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed in-situ pH 10-11 in warm alkaline rock pores, not a black-smoker core. Screen 1 hour to 10 days. Measure substrate concentration after delivery and dilution; continue directly to rxn_017 in the same fluid."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide addition to the nitrile, proton transfer, and tautomerization yield prolinamide.", "reasoning": "Base-promoted nitrile hydration is a real alternative mechanism to acidic hydration. No mineral catalyst or hydrogen supply is assumed. Useful flux at the proposed alkalinity is not established, and thermal degradation can compete. Buffer capacity and in-situ pH must be measured rather than inferred from room-temperature vent-fluid values."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Alkaline prolinamide hydrolysis to racemic proline", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_016"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "415-435 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar; liquid water", "catalyst": "Hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same in-situ pH 10-11 compartment as rxn_016. Include conversion to carboxylate in the alkalinity budget. Cool the entire reacted fluid after the selected endpoint; no acid-finished amide handoff is required."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide-mediated amide cleavage gives prolinate and ammonia; neutral-parent products are used for graph bookkeeping.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis is chemically credible, but the competition between its rate and high-temperature substrate loss requires measurement. Explicit balance: 2 mol_013 + 2 mol_002 -> mol_017 + mol_018 + 2 mol_016. No preferential formation or preservation of L-proline is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Alkaline iminohydantoin hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_016"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "415-435 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar; liquid water", "catalyst": "Hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed in-situ pH 10-11; same compartment as rxn_022 and rxn_023. Screen within a total 1 hour to 10 day exposure and determine iminohydantoin recovery after delivery."}, "mechanism": "Water/hydroxide addition and proton-transfer-assisted ammonia elimination convert the exocyclic imino group into a carbonyl.", "reasoning": "This is a plausible hydrolytic transformation, not a metal reduction. Amidines can resist alkaline hydrolysis, so the productive rate of this fused substrate is a specific uncertainty. Higher temperature cannot be assumed to solve that problem without increasing competing decomposition."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Alkaline hydration of N-carbamoylprolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "415-435 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar; liquid water", "catalyst": "Hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed in-situ pH 10-11; same compartment as rxn_020 or rxn_021. Measure the inlet organic concentration after mixing, and screen total exposure from 1 hour to 10 days."}, "mechanism": "Base-promoted nitrile hydration produces N-carbamoylprolinamide.", "reasoning": "The nitrile-hydration class is credible, but retention of the N-carbamoyl group is conditional. Cyclization, decarbamoylation, and degradation can compete. Subsequent reactions occur in the same fluid, so the pathway does not rely on exporting a transient amide before further hydrolysis."}, {"id": "rxn_020", "name": "Alkaline hydrolysis of N-carbamoylprolinamide", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "415-435 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar; liquid water", "catalyst": "Hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same in-situ pH 10-11 compartment as rxn_019 and rxn_023; no intervening acidification. Include carboxylate formation in the fluid's alkalinity balance."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide-mediated carboxamide cleavage releases ammonia and produces N-carbamoylprolinate.", "reasoning": "A terminal carboxamide can hydrolyze without requiring reduction or cleavage of the pyrrolidine ring. Relative rates of carboxamide hydrolysis, ureido-group cleavage, and thermal loss remain unmeasured. This node represents a possible channel rather than guaranteed selective accumulation."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "Thermal cyclodeamination of N-carbamoylprolinamide in alkaline fluid", "inputs": ["mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_016"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "415-435 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar; liquid water", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Same in-situ pH 10-11 compartment as rxn_019. Treat this as a potentially minor competing channel, not an independently optimized or selectively isolated stage."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular attack of the terminal ureido nitrogen on the carboxamide, with proton transfer and ammonia loss, would close the hydantoin ring.", "reasoning": "This is the weakest enumerated channel. Five-membered closure is structurally possible, but amide leaving-group chemistry is unfavorable and alkaline hydrolysis may dominate completely. No effective mineral catalyst is asserted. Productive cyclodeamination under these conditions is speculative; P8 must be rejected if kinetic testing finds negligible hydantoin flux."}, {"id": "rxn_022", "name": "Alkaline opening of proline hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "415-435 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar; liquid water", "catalyst": "Hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed in-situ pH 10-11; continue to rxn_023 in the same compartment. Screen within a total 1 hour to 10 day exposure and measure both remaining hydantoin and ring-opened material."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide attacks the hydantoin carbonyl adjacent to the alpha carbon; acyl-nitrogen cleavage opens the added ring to N-carbamoylprolinate.", "reasoning": "Base-promoted hydantoin ring opening is a well-founded reaction class and is mechanistically distinct from acidic opening. Nevertheless, useful rates for this fused substrate at geochemically available alkalinity are not established. No enzymatic hydantoinase activity is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_023", "name": "Alkaline thermal decarbamoylation to racemic proline", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_016", "mol_009"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "415-435 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar; liquid water", "catalyst": "Hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same in-situ pH 10-11 compartment as the preceding alkaline reactions. CO2 is mainly bicarbonate/carbonate here. Quantify residual N-carbamoylproline and proline survival before cooling; do not presume complete deprotection."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the N-carbamoyl group releases prolinate, ammonia, and inorganic carbon.", "reasoning": "This is the principal bottleneck of the alkaline protected routes. Nonenzymatic ureido-group removal can be much slower than hydantoin opening, and the proposed window may fail before useful proline release occurs. It is a conditional finishing hypothesis, not a demonstrated environmental conversion. Explicit neutral-parent balance: 2 mol_015 + 2 mol_002 -> mol_017 + mol_018 + 2 mol_016 + 2 mol_009."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Direct cyclic Strecker route with acidic hydrolysis", "description": "The strongest conditional sequence: ring-chain equilibration and cyanide capture are followed by uninterrupted acidic nitrile and amide hydrolysis. No dialdehyde, hydrogenation, or amide transfer is required. L-Proline is one component of the racemic product.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_00