{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T07:32:36.956460", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Proline\nOVERALL STRATEGY: An evidence-limited, connected candidate network centered on cyclic-imine Strecker chemistry. Conditional delivery of 4-aminobutanal supplies an equilibrating 1-pyrroline pool; cyanide capture forms saturated, racemic prolinonitrile before prolonged hydrolysis. This avoids succinaldehyde/Paal-Knorr competition and requires neither unactivated H2 nor speculative Fe(0) reduction. Ten distinct reaction sequences are enumerated: five covalent sequences, each with either an entirely acidic or an entirely alkaline hydrolysis module. These are not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses. Direct prolinonitrile hydrolysis is the strongest conditional route; cyanate-derived and Bucherer-Bergs-type hydantoin branches are less secure, particularly their substrate-specific cyclization and deprotection kinetics. All stereogenic chemistry is achiral, so L-proline is obtained together with D-proline, not enantiopure. The environmental supply of 4-aminobutanal and concentrated cyanate remains unestablished. Numerical conditions below are proposed screening envelopes, not experimentally established productive windows. Acidic modules operate in one acidified surface compartment; alkaline modules operate in one warm alkaline hydrothermal compartment. No pathway requires harvesting prolinamide between acidic and alkaline hydrolysis stages. Productive operation still requires measurements of branching, carbon recovery, residence time, dilution, and buffering capacity. Formulae use neutral-parent bookkeeping; actual protonation, carbonate, cyanate, and ammonia speciation must be calculated at the stated pH. Unspecified stereochemistry denotes racemic precursor pools. environment_formed records a primary formation setting; alternative settings are specified in reaction nodes. Non-target terminal coproducts use the schema's intermediate role.\n\nMOLECULES (18 nodes):\n  - mol_001: C4H9NO (Gamma-aminobutyraldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_003: C4H7N (1-Pyrroline) [intermediate]\n  - mol_004: CHN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_005: C5H8N2 (Prolinonitrile, racemic pool) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_006: CHNO (Isocyanic acid; neutral parent of cyanate) [starting]\n  - mol_007: C6H9N3O (N-Carbamoylprolinonitrile, racemic pool) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C6H9N3O (Proline-derived iminohydantoin, racemic pool) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_009: CO2 (Carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_010: C6H8N2O2 (Prolinonitrile N-carbamic acid, racemic pool) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C6H8N2O2 (Proline-derived iminooxazolidinone, racemic pool) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C6H8N2O2 (Proline hydantoin, racemic pool) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C5H10N2O (Prolinamide, racemic pool) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C6H11N3O2 (N-Carbamoylprolinamide, racemic pool) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C6H10N2O3 (N-Carbamoylproline, racemic pool) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_016: H3N (Ammonia) [intermediate]\n  - mol_017: C5H9NO2 (L-Proline) [target]\n  - mol_018: C5H9NO2 (D-Proline) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (23 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001 -> mol_003, mol_002 (Intramolecular condensation of 4-aminobutanal) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_003, mol_004 -> mol_005 (Cyclic-imine Strecker capture) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_005, mol_006 -> mol_007 (Cyanate carbamoylation of prolinonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_007 -> mol_008 (Cyclization of N-carbamoylprolinonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_005, mol_009 -> mol_010 (Reversible N-carboxylation of prolinonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_010 -> mol_011 (Carbamate cyclization to an iminooxazolidinone) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_011 -> mol_012 (Iminooxazolidinone rearrangement to proline hydantoin) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_005, mol_002 -> mol_013 (Acidic hydration of prolinonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_013, mol_002 -> mol_017, mol_018, mol_016 (Acidic prolinamide hydrolysis to racemic proline) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_008, mol_002 -> mol_012, mol_016 (Acidic iminohydantoin hydrolysis) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_007, mol_002 -> mol_014 (Acidic hydration of N-carbamoylprolinonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_014, mol_002 -> mol_015, mol_016 (Acidic hydrolysis of N-carbamoylprolinamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_013: mol_014 -> mol_012, mol_016 (Acid-assisted cyclodeamination of N-carbamoylprolinamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_014: mol_012, mol_002 -> mol_015 (Acidic opening of proline hydantoin) [Surface]\n  - rxn_015: mol_015, mol_002 -> mol_017, mol_018, mol_016, mol_009 (Acidic decarbamoylation to racemic proline) [Surface]\n  - rxn_016: mol_005, mol_002 -> mol_013 (Alkaline hydration of prolinonitrile) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_017: mol_013, mol_002 -> mol_017, mol_018, mol_016 (Alkaline prolinamide hydrolysis to racemic proline) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_018: mol_008, mol_002 -> mol_012, mol_016 (Alkaline iminohydantoin hydrolysis) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_019: mol_007, mol_002 -> mol_014 (Alkaline hydration of N-carbamoylprolinonitrile) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_020: mol_014, mol_002 -> mol_015, mol_016 (Alkaline hydrolysis of N-carbamoylprolinamide) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_021: mol_014 -> mol_012, mol_016 (Thermal cyclodeamination of N-carbamoylprolinamide in alkaline fluid) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_022: mol_012, mol_002 -> mol_015 (Alkaline opening of proline hydantoin) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_023: mol_015, mol_002 -> mol_017, mol_018, mol_016, mol_009 (Alkaline thermal decarbamoylation to racemic proline) [Hydrothermal]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Direct cyclic Strecker route with acidic hydrolysis (4 steps)\n  - P2: Direct cyclic Strecker route with alkaline hydrolysis (4 steps)\n  - P3: Cyanate capture and iminohydantoin formation with acidic finishing (7 steps)\n  - P4: Cyanate capture and iminohydantoin formation with alkaline finishing (7 steps)\n  - P5: Open-chain ureido-substituent hydrolysis with acidic finishing (6 steps)\n  - P6: Open-chain ureido-substituent hydrolysis with alkaline finishing (6 steps)\n  - P7: Hydration-before-hydantoin cyclization with acidic finishing (7 steps)\n  - P8: Hydration-before-hydantoin cyclization with alkaline finishing (7 steps)\n  - P9: Bucherer-Bergs-type carbonate branch with acidic finishing (7 steps)\n  - P10: Bucherer-Bergs-type carbonate branch with alkaline finishing (7 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 23 reactions | 19 balanced as written | 4 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_009 balanceable: 2 C5H10N2O + 2 H2O -> C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 2 H3N\n  - rxn_015 balanceable: 2 C6H10N2O3 + 2 H2O -> C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 2 H3N + 2 CO2\n  - rxn_017 balanceable: 2 C5H10N2O + 2 H2O -> C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 2 H3N\n  - rxn_023 balanceable: 2 C6H10N2O3 + 2 H2O -> C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 2 H3N + 2 CO2\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"target_achievement\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"feedback\": \"<pathway-specific feedback>\",\n            \"approved\": <true if confidence >= 0.8>,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_ids with issues\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\"improvements\"]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"<id of best pathway>\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"<overall assessment>\"\n}\n\nNote: \"environmental_progression\" scores Environmental Consistency (conditions fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.81\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.84,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.92,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.6,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.81,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.78,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is the strongest conditional route. Aminoaldehyde cyclization, cyclic-imine cyanide capture, and successive nitrile/amide hydrolysis form a chemically credible sequence. However, an acidified surface compartment does not by itself establish sufficient acidity, temperature, or residence time for productive hydrolysis. Accumulation of prolinonitrile before hydrolysis also requires measurement. The flagged reactions have operating-evidence gaps, not conservation violations. Racemic production includes the requested L-proline and is not intrinsically a target failure.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Provide concentrations, pH, temperature, residence time, and HCN-retention conditions for imine capture and the subsequent acidic module.\",\n        \"Measure the complete acidic hydrolysis time course without requiring isolation of prolinamide, including substrate loss and carbon recovery.\",\n        \"Confirm proline identity and quantify both enantiomers by chiral analysis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.76,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.78\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.82,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.91,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.55,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.78,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.74,\n      \"feedback\": \"The alkaline direct route is chemically coherent and can produce racemic proline without switching hydrolysis regimes or harvesting prolinamide. Its principal uncertainties are whether the specified hydrothermal fluid is sufficiently alkaline and hot for useful conversion, and whether surface-generated prolinonitrile reaches that fluid without excessive dilution or loss. Those operating and transport details are not supplied.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Measure nitrile and amide hydrolysis together at explicitly stated alkaline hydrothermal conditions.\",\n        \"Specify a physically plausible t