ide hydrolysis, and final urea cleavage within one alkaline compartment.\",\n        \"Quantify transfer losses and establish that productive concentrations persist after mixing with hydrothermal fluid.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.73,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.77,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.55,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.7,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.65\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.73,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.8,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.52,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.59,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.62,\n      \"feedback\": \"The proposed structures can support a balanced route, but rxn_013 is a substantial kinetic uncertainty. Converting a ureido-amide into a fused hydantoin by ammonia loss requires productive intramolecular acyl substitution involving a weak nucleophile and an unactivated amide. Competing amide hydrolysis may instead feed P5. Subsequent acidic ring opening and N-decarbamoylation also require demonstrated operating windows.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Measure rxn_013 against competing rxn_012 under the same acidic conditions.\",\n        \"Confirm fused-hydantoin formation structurally rather than assigning it from ammonia release or molecular formula alone.\",\n        \"Demonstrate net proline accumulation through the entire acidic module, including possible hydantoin re-formation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.73,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.74,\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.43,\n        \"rxn_022\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_023\": 0.49\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.7,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.76,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.46,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.43,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.53,\n      \"feedback\": \"This branch contains two major unresolved steps. Thermal cyclodeamination in alkaline water, rxn_021, cannot be inferred from heating alone: amide hydrolysis competes, and any hydantoin formed is exposed to ring-opening conditions. The final alkaline N-decarbamoylation is independently uncertain. These concerns weaken this particular sequence without invalidating the alternative open-chain route through rxn_020.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_021\",\n        \"rxn_023\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Determine the relative rates of rxn_020, rxn_021, and rxn_022 under identical alkaline conditions.\",\n        \"Use direct structural measurements to establish whether hydantoin is a productive intermediate rather than a trace side product.\",\n        \"Demonstrate nonenzymatic N-decarbamoylation with acceptable proline recovery under the same environmental constraints.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.62,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.51,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.53,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.7,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.65\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.68,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.77,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.5,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.51,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.57,\n      \"feedback\": \"The carbonate branch is a plausible mechanistic hypothesis, but conventional Bucherer-Bergs chemistry does not by itself demonstrate this extension to a cyclic secondary aminonitrile. Reversible N-carboxylation must generate sufficient productive flux, followed by substrate-specific cyclization and rearrangement. Mol_010, mol_011, and mol_012 sha